hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6656","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: There is no charity in working cattle."
"6657","From al-Thawri, from Yala ibn Ata, from Abdullah ibn Muslim, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: There is no charity on a working bull, nor on the camel of a litter charity."
"6658","From Mamar, from Qatadah, on the working she-camel: When they are five camels, therein a sheep."
"6659","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a man has a train of camels he works upon, therein is charity."
"6660","From Mamar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no charity on working cattle."
"6661","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: There is nothing in the working one."
"6662","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri on working camels: In every five a sheep."
"6663","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Mujahid, that he said: When a man has forty sheep in a town he milks, there is no zakat upon him, meaning household ones. And Sufyan said: And our saying is likewise: If he bought them for carrying, and a year passed over them, there is no zakat therein; and goats and camels are of that station."
"6664","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, that he used to say: When two partners work their wealth, their wealth is not combined in charities. And I informed Ata of the saying of Tawus in that, and he said: I see it but true."
"6665","From al-Thawri, he said: Our saying: Nothing is due upon two partners unless forty is complete for this one, and forty for this one."
"6666","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When their shepherd is one, and they water together, and return together, and pasture together, they are collected together."
"6667","Mamar and al-Thawri informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Masruq, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen and ordered him to take of every thirty cattle a tabii or tabiah, and of every forty a musinnah, and of every adult a dinar, or its worth in Muafir garments."
"6668","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali: In cattle, in thirty a tabii or tabiah, and in forty a musinnah."
"6669","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Tawus informed him, that Muadh ibn Jabal said: I shall not take of the lesser numbers of cattle anything until I come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered therein something."
"6670","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: Muadh ibn Jabal kept at al-Janad when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen until he died, and Abu Bakr, then he came to Umar and he returned him to what he was upon."
"6671","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said: For thirty cows, a jadha tabi (a young bull calf) is due, and for forty cows, a cow is due. He said: And I did not hear anything from him beyond that."
"6672","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: The workers of Ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn Awf and his workers, used to take one cow for every fifty cows, and two cows for eighty. Then when they became many, one cow for every fifty cows. I said: Which cow? He said: Just so."
"6673","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Salih ibn Dinar informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Suwayd to take a tabi for every thirty cows, and a cow for every forty cows, adding nothing beyond that. He said: So Uthman ordered his workers to take that, and when the cows became many and exceeded that, then for every thirty cows a tabi, and for every forty cows a musinnah (mature cow)."
"6674","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Muadh that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the awqas, what is between thirty and forty, and what is between forty and fifty, and he said: There is nothing due in them."
"6675","From al-Thawri, from Firas, from al-Shabi, he said: There is nothing due in the awqas between thirty and forty, and there is nothing in less than thirty. And Ibrahim said: There is nothing in less than thirty."
"6676","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: There is nothing due in less than thirty cows; when they reach thirty, a jadha tabi or jadhah is due in them, until they reach forty; when they reach forty, a musinnah cow is due in them; and in what is above that of cows, for every thirty a tabi, and for every forty a musinnah."
"6677","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, he said: For thirty, a tabiah is due, and for every forty a musinnah; and there is nothing in between forty and sixty; and for sixty, two tabiahs or two tabis; and for seventy, a musinnah and a tabi; and for eighty, two musinnahs; and for ninety, three atabi; and for one hundred, two tabis and a musinnah; and for one hundred and ten, two musinnahs and a tabi; and for one hundred and twenty, three musinnahs. Its young and old are counted, and buffaloes are counted with cows. Whatever cows are for trade are assessed by value; they are not taken on this calculation, but are assessed by value, and when it reaches two hundred dirhams, zakat is due in it."
"6678","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: For every five cows a sheep is due, and for ten two sheep, and for fifteen three sheep, and for every twenty four sheep. Al-Zuhri said: When they are twenty-five, a cow is due in them up to seventy-five; when they exceed seventy-five, two cows are due up to one hundred and twenty; when they exceed one hundred and twenty, for every forty cows a cow is due. That was a concession for the people of Yemen; then after that this was not reported."
"6679","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: I used to hear for a time that they used to say: Take from us what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took. And I used to wonder when they did not accept that from them, until al-Zuhri narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote a document containing these obligations, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away before he wrote to the workers; so Abu Bakr acted upon it and carried it out after him according to what he wrote. I do not know but that he mentioned cows also."
"6680","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The obligations of cows are like the obligations of camels, except for the ages in them."
"6681","From Mamar, he said: Simak ibn al-Fadl gave me a letter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Malik ibn Kaflanis, and I read it, and in it was: In what is watered by the sky and rivers, a tenth is due; and in what is irrigated by drawing water, half a tenth; and in cows, like camels."
"6682","From Malik, from Humayd ibn Qays, from Tawus, from Muadh ibn Jabal that he took from cows, from thirty a tabi, and from forty a musinnah. And they asked him about less than thirty, and he said: I did not hear anything about it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not command me anything concerning it."
"6683","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: For every thirty cows a tabi, and for every forty cows a musinnah."
"6684","Mamar informed us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has camels and does not pay their due, or he said: their sadaqah, he will be laid flat for them on the Day of Resurrection on a wide plain, on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, they will trample him with their feet and bite him with their mouths; their first will be brought back to their last, until judgment is passed among the people, then he will see his way. And whoever has sheep and does not pay their due, he will be laid flat for them on the Day of Resurrection on a wide plain, on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, they will trample him with their hooves and butt him with their horns; their first will be brought back upon their last, until judgment is passed among the people, then he will see his way. And whoever has gold or silver and does not pay its due, it will be made into plates of fire on the Day of Resurrection and placed on his side, his back, and his forehead, until judgment is passed among the people, then he will see his way. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar."
"6685","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: Excellent camels are thirty camels: their sadaqah is paid, their noble one is ridden, their fat one is slaughtered, and their abundant-milking one is lent. He said: And did it reach you concerning that: milking on the day of watering in camels? He said: I do not think so. And he said: If there is no surplus in the camels beyond the needs of their owners, then do not milk on the day they come to water."
"6686","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Abu Hurayrah said: Excellent wealth is thirty camels."
"6687","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: Whoever has camels and does not give the right of Allah in them, they will come on the Day of Resurrection in their worst form, trampling him with their feet. It was said: And what is their right? He said: And he mentioned four things. Abd Allah said: I do not know with which of them he began. He said: They are milked at the watering place, their backs are lent for riding, their fat ones are slaughtered, and their milking ones are lent."
"6688","Mamar informed us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Salih, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has wealth and does not pay its right, a bald serpent with two black spots over its eyes will be made for him; it will follow him until it puts his hand into its mouth, and it will keep gnawing it until judgment is passed among the servants."
"6689","From Mamar, from Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Muawiyah, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever his master asks him for surplus of his wealth and he does not give it to him, it will be turned into a bald serpent on the Day of Resurrection."
"6690","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Abu Dharr, he said: Give glad tidings of a branding on the foreheads, the sides, and the backs to the hoarders of treasures."
"6691","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no owner of camels who does not act in them with their right but that they will come on the Day of Resurrection more numerous than they ever were, and he will be seated for them on a wide plain, running upon him with their legs and feet. And no owner of cows who does not act in them with their right but that they will come on the Day of Resurrection more numerous than they ever were, and he will be seated for them on a wide plain, butting him with their horns and trampling him with their legs. And no owner of sheep who does not act in them with their right but that they will come on the Day of Resurrection more numerous than they ever were, and he will be seated for them on a wide plain, butting him with their horns and trampling him with their hooves, with no hornless one among them, nor one with a broken horn. And no owner of a treasure who does not act in it with its right but that his treasure will come on the Day of Resurrection as a bald serpent following him with its mouth open; when it comes to him he will flee from it, and it will call to him: Take your treasure which you hid, for I have no need of it. When he sees that there is no escape from it, he will put his hand into its mouth and it will gnaw it like the gnawing of a stallion. Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say this statement, then we asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari about that, and he said the like of the statement of Ubayd."
"6692","And Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the right of camels? He said: Milking them at the water, lending their bucket, lending their stallion, lending their milk, and carrying upon them in the path of Allah."
"6693","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata al-Khurasani narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: In sheep there is of the right like what is in camels."
"6694","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me that a man from Banu Nahd said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man of much wealth. He said: How much is your wealth? He said: The valley in which I stay cannot contain it. He said: How are you at the manihah (lending a milch animal)? He said: One hundred every year. He said: How are you at the lending of its camels in heat? He said: The camels go out and the people go out, and whoever wishes to take a camel takes one. He said: How are you at hospitality? He said: I cling, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, to the old she-camel, the aged one, the great one, and the udder. He said: Is your wealth more beloved to you or the wealth of your clients? He said: No, rather my wealth. He said: Your wealth from your wealth is only what you ate and consumed, or wore and wore out, or spent and sent forth; and what remains is for your clients."
"6695","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Excellent wealth is thirty camels: the abundant-milking one is lent, the fat one is slaughtered, the stallion covers, and the back is lent. Thirty is better than forty. And woe to the owners of two hundred: how many of its rights they do not fulfill."
"6696","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Whoever has camels and does not pay their right, they will come on the Day of Resurrection in their worst form, trampling him with their feet. It was said: And what is their right? He said: Lend to the people, lend the back, milk at the watering place, slaughter the fat one; I think he said: And the stallion is lent for covering."
"6697","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyab that he was asked about donkeys: Is there zakat in them? He said: No, even if they reach such and such, a great number, two hundred or three hundred. Sufyan said: And we say: Unless they are for trade."
"6698","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: The Muslims used to like, when one of them acquired wealth, to pay its zakat; and when a year passed over his wealth, to pay zakat with it for what a year had not passed over of his wealth."
"6699","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whoever acquires wealth pays its zakat with his wealth; and when he acquires wealth he pays its zakat when he acquires it, with his wealth. The Muslims used to like that."
"6700","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Irak ibn Malik that he said: We sought the knowledge of sadaqah, and I did not see anyone more knowledgeable about it than some of its people. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pay them sadaqah, from Juhaynah and Ghifar and others. He said: I said to them: A man buys livestock, then the collector comes to him the next day? They said: He pays its sadaqah to whoever finds it. Consider the one who sold it before the collector came, and the next day came: does the one who sold it pay sadaqah? I said: No. So it is likewise."
"6701","From Mamar, he said: I said to al-Zuhri: Livestock which a man pays sadaqah for, keeping it eleven months, then he sells it. He said: The sadaqah is upon the buyer. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, the like of it."
"6702","From Mamar, from a man to whom the collector came, while his livestock had reached thirty-nine sheep, counting them count by count, until after he passed, a sheep of them gave birth, while the collector had turned away. He said: They say: There is no sadaqah in them. Mamar said: And I say: When the principal had already paid zakat, that is better. I say: When they were one hundred and nineteen sheep which the collector counts, and he took a sheep from them, then its principal has now paid sadaqah; if he turns away and a sheep of them gives birth, there is no sadaqah in it until a year passes. He said: And it pleases me concerning the thirty-nine in which the collector turned away, then it gave birth, that its sadaqah be taken."
"6703","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sadaqah upon the Muslim in his slave nor in his horse."
"6704","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, do you not know that I have exempted the sadaqah of horses and slaves."
"6705","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have waived for you the sadaqah of horses."
"6706","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, that he said: I have exempted the sadaqah of horses and slaves."
"6707","Mamar and Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ismail, from Makhul, from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: There is no sadaqah upon the Muslim in his horse nor in his slave. Abd al-Razzaq said: So I narrated it to Muhammad ibn Rashid, and he informed me that he heard Makhul narrating it from Irak, from Abu Hurayrah."
"6708","From al-Thawri, from Abd Allah ibn Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade taking anything from horses."
"6709","From al-Thawri, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no zakat in pasturing horses."
"6710","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you that in horses, or in any of the riding animals, there is sadaqah? He said: I do not know of it."
"6711","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, he said: There is no zakat in any of the riding animals unless they are for trade, except sheep, camels, and cows."
"6712","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The people of Sham came to Umar and said: Our wealth is only horses and slaves, so take sadaqah from us. He said: I do not wish to take anything that was not taken before me. Then he consulted the people, and Ali said: As long as they willingly agree, it is good, provided it is not a jizyah to be taken as such after you. So Umar took from horses ten dirhams, and from slaves ten dirhams, ten dirhams each year; and he provided for the horses, for every horse ten ajribah each month, and he provided for the slaves two jaribs each month. Mamar said: And I heard other than Abu Ishaq say: When Muawiyah came, he calculated that, and what he gave them was more than what he took from them, so he left them, neither taking from them nor giving them. He said: What is the jarib? He said: An amount of food."
"6713","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi al-Husayn informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him that Uthman used to take sadaqah on horses, and that al-Saib ibn Yazid informed him that he used to bring to Umar ibn al-Khattab the sadaqah of horses. Ibn Abi Husayn said, and Ibn Shihab said: I do not know that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prescribed sadaqah on horses."
"6714","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr informed me that Yahya ibn Yala informed him that he heard Yala ibn Umayyah say: Abd al-Rahman ibn Umayyah, the brother of Yala ibn Umayyah, bought a mare from a man of Yemen for one hundred qalus (young she-camels). The seller regretted and caught up with Umar, saying: Yala and his brother seized a mare of mine. So he wrote to Yala to come to him, and he came and informed him of the news. Umar said: Do horses reach this price among you? He said: I never knew a horse to reach this before this one. Umar said: Shall we take one sheep from forty sheep, and take nothing from horses? Take one dinar from every horse. He said: So he imposed upon horses a dinar each."
"6715","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father that he used to dislike the sale of animal sadaqah before it was taken into possession, and he saw no harm in food."
"6716","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: As for the sale of food, there is no harm; as for livestock, it is disliked, and it is not riba."
"6717","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Do not buy your sadaqah until it is taken possession of from you."
"6718","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he said to Uthman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Suwayd: I do not think it is lawful for you to sell the sadaqah until you restrain it. So Uthman said to Tawus: This Ibrahim claims that it is not lawful for us to sell the sadaqah until it is restrained. Tawus said, by the Lord of this House, while he was in its shade: It is not lawful for you to sell it before it is restrained, nor after it is restrained. You were not charged with that. But if you must, then restrain them and brand them."
"6719","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that those who passed used to dislike buying their sadaqahs. He said: If you do so after it is taken possession of from you, there is no harm, but I prefer that you not do it."
"6720","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Tawus: Shall I sell the sadaqah before it is restrained? He said: No. I said: Shall you give the buyer the choice? He said: We heard that it is not to be bought until it is restrained."
"6721","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: When the collector comes to you, hand over your sadaqah to him and do not buy it from him; leave to him what he undertakes. By Allah, they say: We will leave it for you, and I say: No. Then they say: Buy it, and we say: No, it is only for Allah."
"6722","From al-Thawri, from Yala ibn Ata, from Muslim ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, I said: An obligatory camel which I reckon for the collector and restrain, shall I buy it? He said: May Allah not bless it; do not buy the purification of your wealth."
"6723","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah forbid the sale of sadaqah before it is paid out."
"6724","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from more than one, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade buying the sadaqah until it is restrained and branded."
"6725","From Yahya ibn al-Ala al-Bajali, from Jahdam ibn Abd Allah, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of sadaqahs until they are taken into possession."
"6726","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani informed me, from Asim ibn Damrah, he informed him that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib say: For five camels a sheep is due; if it is not found, the age below it is taken, and the owner of the livestock is fined two sheep or ten dirhams."
"6727","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Damrah, from Ali, he said: When the collector takes in camels an age above the due age, he returns to them ten dirhams or two sheep; and when he takes an age below the due age, they return to him ten dirhams; and when he takes in place of a bint labun a male ibn labun, then ten dirhams or two sheep."
"6728","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When the collector finds an age above the due age or below the due age, they return to him in place of the difference between them twenty dirhams or two sheep. Al-Thawri said: And this is only in camels; when they are for trade, they are assessed in dirhams."
"6729","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When the lower age is not found, the higher age is taken, and two sheep or ten dirhams are returned to the owner of the livestock."
"6730","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari that Umar wrote to one of his workers not to take from a man who does not find in his camels the due age except that age from similar camels of his, or a just value."
"6731","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ibn Tawus: I was informed that you say: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: When the due age is not found, then its value. He said: I never said it. He said: I said: Then he gives whatever he wishes? He said: Perhaps I said it, but I heard nothing from him about it."
"6732","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a collector, and he found due upon a man a bint makhad. The man said: I will not give, in the first sadaqah taken from me, a she-camel with no back in it nor belly, or he said: udder; but choose a she-camel. He said: So the collector mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Inform him of his due right; if he volunteers something, accept it from him. Hushaym said: And al-Hajjaj informed me, from Ata, similar to this, except that he said the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Inform him of his due right; if he volunteers something, accept it from him."
"6733","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to a man among those who had accepted Islam, intending to take from him the due age of sadaqah taken from him, and he said to him: Do not leave an age better than the due age to take; for no collector for Allah has stood over it before you."
"6734","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that the workers of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take sadaqah from the people at their waters and in their courtyards."
"6735","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus said to me: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: They are approached wherever they are."
"6736","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to one of his workers: Call the people with their wealth to the most convenient gathering places for them, and the nearest to their interests; and do not detain the people, their first upon their last; for staying long is severe upon livestock, destructive to it; and do not drive them in a driving that takes pasture and water far from them."
"6737","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The people used not to delay their sadaqah, in drought nor plenty, nor leanness nor fatness, until Muawiyah came and delayed it for them, charging them with it."
"6738","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus said to me: I used to say: Fear Allah, for two sadaqahs are due upon you; if they give me one I take it, or two I take."
"6739","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, from Tawus, that he said: If two sadaqahs coincide, only the first is taken, like the jizyah."
"6740","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: For me to have asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who withholds his sadaqah, and he said: I place it in its place, is he to be fought? That is more beloved to me than red camels. He said: And Abu Bakr held that he is to be fought."
"6741","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, he said: When Abu Bakr prepared, or he said: when Abu Bakr set out to fight the people of apostasy, he said to him: How do you fight the people, O Abu Bakr, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say it, they protect from me their blood and wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I shall surely fight whoever separates prayer from zakat, for zakat is the right of wealth. By Allah, if they withheld from me a tether which they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would fight them for it. Umar said: By Allah, it was only that I saw that Allah had opened the breast of Abu Bakr for fighting, and I knew it was the truth."
"6742","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you permit me to place the sadaqah of my wealth in its places, or to the rulers necessarily? He said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: When you place it in its places, so long as you do not give anyone from it anything you yourself dictate, there is no harm. I heard it from him more than once, reporting it from Ibn Abbas."
"6743","He said: And Ata said to me: And Ibn Umar used to say: Pay zakat to the rulers. He said: A man said to him while disputing with him: They do not place it in its places. He said: Even so."
"6744","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A hadith was narrated, raised to Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged the people to give sadaqah, and he was approached, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abu Jahm, and Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), have withheld sadaqah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He resents us only because he was poor and Allah and His Messenger enriched him. As for Khalid ibn al-Walid, he has kept his armors and his gear in the path of Allah. As for Abbas, the uncle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is upon him and the like of it with it."
"6745","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Nuaym informed me that Ibn Muti said: I will not pay the sadaqah of my wealth to Ibn al-Zubayr, who feeds it to his horses and feeds it to his slaves. So Ibn Umar sent to him: You have not done right and have not paid it; even if you give the like of it in charity, it will not be accepted from you. Pay it to them, for you were not commanded to pay it except to them, whether righteous or sinful."
"6746","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if the sadaqah were placed in its places, shall I place it myself in its places, or pay it to the rulers? He said without hesitation: That is not for you when they place it in its places. I then said: It was only that Ibn Abbas said that because they do not place it in its places? He said: Yes. And he said the like concerning zakat al-fitr, and every sadaqah of livestock or crops. He said: And it will suffice you to pay it to them, earning you the reward, while they bear what they undertake."
"6747","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he said to Tawus: We have lands; shall we place their sadaqah in its places or pay it to them? He said: If you can take them by their hands, do so. And Ibn al-Musayyab said: If, when you place it in its places, you do not weaken thereby your authority in it, in what is necessary of stipends and frontiers, there is no harm; otherwise not."
"6748","From Mamar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, he said: Wealth accumulated with me. He said: So I went to Ibn Umar, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Said al-Khudri, and Sad ibn Abi Waqqas. I came to each of them alone and said that wealth has accumulated with me, and that these rulers place it where you see, and I have found a place for it; what do you think? All of them said: Pay it to them."
"6749","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He does not pay to them when they do not place it in its places."
"6750","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: A man came to Ibn Umar and said: I have wealth, shall I pay its zakat? Ibn Umar said: Fie upon the far one. They said: He says: I have wealth, where shall I place its zakat? He said: Why does he not say so? A heap of the heaps of Hell came to me, upon him a black cloak of dog hair: Pay it to your rulers, even if they tear the flesh of dogs upon their tables. Mamar said: So I mentioned that to Hammad, and he denied that Ibn Umar said it."
"6751","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: A man came to Ibn Umar asking him about the zakat of his wealth, and he said: Pay it to the ruler. He said: Our rulers are the dihqans. He said: And what are the dihqans? He said: Of the polytheists. He said: Then do not pay it to the polytheists."
"6752","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Zakat was paid in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and to whom he appointed for it; and in the time of Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman likewise. Then the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed concerning it."
"6753","From Abd Allah ibn Muharrar, he said: Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, he said: I entered upon Ibn Umar, I and an elder older than I. He said: I think he said: Ibn al-Musayyab. So I asked him about sadaqah: Shall I pay it to the rulers? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Even if they buy with it cheetahs and falcons? He said: Yes. So I said to the elder when we went out: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No. And I said to Maymun ibn Mihran: Do you say what Ibn Umar said? He said: No."
"6754","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Aban informed me, he said: I entered upon al-Hasan while he was in hiding in the time of al-Hajjaj, in the house of Abu Khalifah. A man said to him: I asked Ibn Umar: Shall I pay zakat to the rulers? Ibn Umar said: Place it among the poor and needy. He said: Al-Hasan said to me: Did I not tell you that Ibn Umar, when he trusted a man, said: Place it among the poor and needy."
"6755","From Mamar, from Ayyub, he said: I never asked al-Hasan about anything as I asked him about this. He said: At one time he would say to me: Pay it to them; and at one time he would say to me: Do not pay it to them."
"6756","From al-Numan ibn Abi Shaybah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: Whatever they take from you, count it as reward."
"6757","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, I heard him say: Do not pay it to them, meaning the rulers."
"6758","From al-Thawri, he said: Ibn Abbas, Ibn al-Musayyab, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, Ibrahim al-Nakhai, Muhammad ibn Ali Abu Jafar, and Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman used to say: Do not pay zakat to one who is unjust in it. Sufyan said: And al-Hasan, Ibrahim ibn Ali, and Hammad used to say: What is taken from you of its zakat, count it as reward. And it is the saying of al-Thawri, he says: If they compel you, it suffices you, but do not pay it to them voluntarily. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard Mamar say: What they take from you suffices you, and what is hidden from them, place it in its places."
"6759","From Mamar, he said: I heard a freedman of Anas called Abd al-Aziz, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: What they take from you suffices you. He said: And it reached me from Ibn al-Musayyab the like of that."
"6760","From Ibn al-Mubarak, and he is Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani, from Hisham, the companion of al-Dastuwai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, that Abu Bakr said, in what he enjoined upon Umar: Whoever pays zakat to other than its deserving ones, his zakat is not accepted, even if he gives all the world in charity; and whoever fasts the month of Ramadan in other than it, his fast is not accepted from him, even if he fasts the whole age."
"6761","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim, that al-Hasan and Ibrahim said: What they take from you, count it as reward; and what is hidden with you, place it in its places. And it is the saying of Mamar and al-Thawri."
"6762","From Mamar, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who sent his zakat with a man to pay it to the ruler, and it perished on the way: Does it suffice him? He said: So he laughed and said: What are you, O people of Basrah, but a piece of the people of Sham; you settled among the people of Iraq. It does not suffice him, even if it arrives also. It is in the position of a debt. He said: I said to him: But Ibn Umar said: Pay to them, even if they tear the flesh of dogs upon their tables. He said: Allah forbid that Ibn Umar said that."
"6763","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When he sends his zakat of wealth and it perishes, does it suffice him? Mamar said: Hammad said: It does not suffice him, even if it arrives."
"6764","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: When a man takes out his zakat and it is stolen, he is liable for it; it is in the position of a debt. Al-Thawri said: And Hammad said it. Sufyan said, and another saying is more beloved to me: That there is no liability in it so long as he has not set it aside for it, or turned it over in something."
"6765","From Mamar, from al-Thawri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sadaqah is not lawful for Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor for the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6766","From Mamar, he said: Muhammad ibn Ziyad informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was distributing dates of sadaqah, and al-Hasan ibn Ali was in his lap. When he finished, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried him upon his shoulder, and his saliva flowed upon the cheek of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his head toward him, and there was a date in his mouth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put his hand in and pulled it out from him, then said to him: Do you not know that sadaqah is not lawful for the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6767","From al-Thawri, from Abu Jahdam Salim al-Basri, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us, and I do not say: forbade you, to mount a donkey upon a mare; and he commanded us to perform ablution fully, and not to eat sadaqah."
"6768","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: Umm Kulthum, daughter of Ali, narrated to me, he said: And I brought to her a sadaqah which had been ordered; she said: I warn our youths, for Maymun or Mihran, the freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed me that he passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: O Maymun, or O Mihran, we are a household forbidden from sadaqah, and our freedmen are from ourselves, so do not eat sadaqah."
"6769","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Hayyan al-Taymi, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Arqam say: It was said to him: Who are the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Those for whom sadaqah is forbidden. It was said: Who are they? He said: The family of Ali, the family of Aqil, the family of Jafar, and the family of Abbas."
"6770","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I enter my house and find a date lying upon my bed; were it not that I fear it is from sadaqah, I would eat it."
"6771","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was narrated from Shahr ibn Hawshab that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted a hair from the ground between his two fingers and said that sadaqah is not lawful for me nor for anyone of my household, not even like this hair."
"6772","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Said informed me, he said: I heard that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent to Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, he said: And a man said to me, and I narrated it to him, rather to Ali ibn al-Husayn, and said: I intended to employ you over the collection of such and such; he said that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that sadaqah is not lawful for Banu Hashim and Banu Abd al-Muttalib. He said: Then whence is your stipend and provision? He did not return anything to him. So I came to Ibn al-Musayyab, and he said to me: What did he say to you? I informed him of his news, and of his saying: Whence is your stipend and provision? He said: Why did you not say: Stipend and provision are only from the fay of the Muslims, wherever you and your companions are; and sadaqah is for its deserving ones."
"6773","From his father Hammam, from Mina, that they came to Ibn Masud in the time of Uthman and said: Give us our stipends. He said: I have no stipend for you. Your stipend is only from your fay and your jizyah, and sadaqah is for its deserving ones. He said: When they kept returning to him, he brought the keys to Uthman, threw them down, and said: I am not a treasurer."
"6774","A man said to al-Thawri: A policeman whose help is sought in something of sadaqah, may he be given from it a dirham or two? He said: No. It is only given from the fay and the jizyah, and sadaqah is for its deserving ones."
"6775","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, both said: Ibn Tawus informed us, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ubadah ibn al-Samit, then said: O Abu al-Walid, do not come on the Day of Resurrection with a young camel with a bellow, a cow with a moo, and a sheep with a bleat. Ubadah said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall never work over anything ever."
"6776","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, that Abu Humayd, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is a man of Banu Saidah, narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ibn al-Utbiyyah, one of al-Azd; when he accounted with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: This is for you and this was gifted to me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not sit in the house of your mother and father, and your gift would come to you if you are truthful. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and addressed us, praised Allah, then said: I employ one of you over work of what Allah has entrusted me with, then one of you comes and says: This is for you and this is a gift gifted to me. Why did he not sit in the house of his father and mother until he sees whether anything is gifted to him or not? By the One in whose hand is my soul, none of you takes anything without its right but that he will meet Allah carrying it on the Day of Resurrection. Let me not know one of you meeting Allah carrying a camel with a bellow, or a cow with a moo, or a sheep with a bleat, bleating. Then he raised his hands until I looked at the whiteness of his armpits, then said: Have I conveyed? The eyes of Abu Humayd saw it, and his ears heard it."
"6777","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abu Humayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ibn al-Utbiyyah, a man of al-Azd, over sadaqah. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accounted with him, he said: This is for you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you not sit in the house of your father and mother, and see whether you are gifted anything or not? Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood as a preacher and said: What is the matter with men whom we appoint over work of what Allah has entrusted us with, then one of them comes and says: This is what is for you, and this was gifted to me. Why did he not sit in the house of his father and mother, and see whether he is gifted anything or not? By the One in whose hand is my soul, none of you misappropriates anything, or he said: anything of that, but that he will come with it on the Day of Resurrection upon his neck: if it was a camel, he will come with it bellowing; if it was a cow, he will come with it mooing; if it was a sheep, he will come with it bleating. Then he raised his hands and said: Have I conveyed? Then he raised his hands until the fairness of his armpit appeared to him. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi similar to it."
"6778","From Mamar, from Ayyub or another, Mamar doubted, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ubadah ibn al-Samit, or Sad ibn Ubadah, and said: Beware lest you come on the Day of Resurrection with a camel carrying it upon your back, with a bellow. He said: I shall not come with it, nor betray it. So he did not work."
"6779","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Muadh to Yemen. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away suddenly, and Abu Bakr was made caliph. He said: And Abu Bakr sent Umar over the pilgrimage season. Muadh came on the day of Arafah with attendants whom he had set aside. Umar met them and said: Who are these? He said: These are for Abu Bakr from the jizyah, and these were gifted to me as a gift. Umar said: Obey me and hand them over to Abu Bakr; if he hands them over to you, you take them. Muadh said: No, by Allah, I shall not do it; I shall not take a gift gifted to me and give it to Abu Bakr. When the next day came, Muadh met Umar and said: I see myself only doing what you told me. I saw myself last night brought to the Fire, with you holding my waistband. So Abu Bakr came to Muadh and he handed them over to him, saying: These were gifted to me, so take them, for you are more deserving of them. He said: So Abu Bakr handed them over to him, and he took them and went with them to his house. Prayer was established, and there they were in the row behind him. When he prayed, he said: Did you pray? They said: Yes. He said: For whom? They said: For Allah. He said: Go, for you are for Allah."
"6780","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Adi ibn Umayrah al-Kindi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: O people, whoever of you we employ over work, and he conceals from us a needle or more, it is misappropriation he will come with on the Day of Resurrection. A black man of the Ansar stood, as if I look at him now, and said: O Messenger of Allah, take back your work from me. He said: And what is that? He said: I heard you say what you said just now. He said: And I say it: Whoever of you we employ over work, let him bring its little and much; what he is given of it he takes, and what he is forbidden he refrains from."
"6781","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And pray over them; indeed your prayer is a comfort for them. Has there reached you any saying to be said when taking sadaqah? He said: No."
"6782","From Abd Allah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, he said: Amr ibn Murrah informed me, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, and he was of the companions of the tree, he said: When people brought sadaqah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, bless them. He said: So my father brought him sadaqah, and he said: O Allah, bless Abu Awfa."
"6783","From Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Madani, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you milk the livestock of another without his permission. Would one of you like his storeroom to be approached, his chest broken, and its food carried away? Their udders store for them their foods. So let no one milk the livestock of another except with his permission. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, and from Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Umar similar to this."
"6784","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: When you are three, appoint one of you, meaning in travel. When you pass by a camel herdsman or a sheep herdsman, call him three times; if anyone answers you, ask him for drink; otherwise alight, milk, and drink, then tie up. I said to him: What is tie up? He said: Tie its udder."
"6785","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Camels we pass by, shall we milk? He said: No, perhaps their owners are in need of them."
"6786","From Ibn Jurayj and al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Umar ibn Kathir, from Ubayd, from Khawlah bint Qays, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Hamzah discussed the world, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The world is green and sweet; whoever takes its right, he is blessed in it. And many a one plunging into the wealth of Allah and the wealth of His Messenger, for him is the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"6787","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata asked about the taghriz of camels. He said: If that is boasting and showing off, then no; if he intends to improve the sale in it, there is no harm. He said: I said: What is their taghriz? He said: He strikes it and pricks it with a stick in its flank."
"6788","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: They asked him about less than thirty of cows, and about honey. He said: I was not commanded anything concerning them."
"6789","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent me to Yemen, and I wanted to take from honey. He said: Al-Mughirah ibn Hakim said to me: There is nothing in it. So I wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz. He said: He spoke the truth, and he is a just, approved man; there is nothing in it."
"6790","From Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz asked me about honey: Is there sadaqah in it? I said: There is no honey in our land, but I asked al-Mughirah ibn Hakim about it, and he said: There is nothing in it. Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: He is a just, trustworthy man; he spoke the truth."
"6791","Salih ibn Dinar informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Uthman ibn Muhammad forbidding him to take sadaqah from honey unless the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it. So Uthman gathered the people of honey, and they testified that Hilal ibn Sad came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with honey. He said: What is this? He said: A gift. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate. Then he came another time and said: What is this? He said: Sadaqah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it and ordered it to be lifted. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention at that time tithes in it, nor half-tithes, except that he took it. Uthman wrote that to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he wrote: You know best. So we used to take whatever they gave of anything, and we did not ask for tithes nor anything; whatever they gave us, we took."
"6792","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I wrote to Ibrahim ibn Maysarah asking him about that, and he wrote to me: Your letter came to me concerning traders. Two men of them came with a letter to Uthman ibn Muhammad, claiming it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), worn out. Uthman ordered and renewed for them the revival of some of the ravines of the people of Tihamah. He said: I think he said: For Qays or Sunbulah. And he mentioned, I think a companion of theirs came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with two skins, one of them sadaqah and one of them a gift. He accepted the gift, and ordered one to take possession of the sadaqah."
"6793","And one of my family whom I do not suspect mentioned to me that he and Urwah al-Sadi discussed in Sham. Urwah claimed that he wrote to Umar asking him about the sadaqah of honey. Urwah claimed that he wrote to him: We have found the explanation of the sadaqah of honey in the land of Taif, so take tithes from it."
"6794","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, he said: Sufyan ibn Abd Allah, the governor of Taif, wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab that those before me ask me to protect a mountain for them, or he said: bees for them. Umar wrote to them: It is only rain flies; no one is more deserving of them than another. If they acknowledge sadaqah to you, protect it for them. He wrote that they acknowledged sadaqah. Umar wrote to him: Protect it for them and take tithes from them."
"6795","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani: That some people of Yemen came to Umar and asked him for a valley, and he gave it to them. They said: O Commander of the Faithful, in it are many bees. He said: Upon you is, for every ten afraq, a firq."
"6796","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri: Concerning the sadaqah of honey, he said: For every ten afraq, a firq."
"6797","From Abd Allah ibn Muharrar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen to take tithes from the people of honey."
"6798","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Abu Sayyarah al-Mutai said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have bees. He said: So pay a tenth from them. He said: I have a mountain, so protect it for me. He said: So he protected it for him."
"6799","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah wrote to me that one of my family whom I do not suspect mentioned to me that he and Urwah ibn Muhammad al-Sadi discussed in Sham about amber. Urwah claimed that he wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz asking him about the sadaqah of amber. Urwah claimed that he wrote to him: Write to me how the earliest people used to take it? Or how was it taken from them? Then write to me. He said: It has been established with me that it came down in the position of spoils, a fifth taken from it. Urwah claimed that he wrote to him to take the fifth, and pay what remains after the fifth to whoever found it. From Layth, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz took a fifth from amber."
"6800","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad asked him about amber, and he said: If there is anything in amber, in it is the fifth."
"6801","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Udhaynah, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: We do not see a fifth in amber, saying: Something cast up by the sea."
"6802","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Urwah ibn Muhammad to ask those before him how the earliest people used to take from amber. He wrote to him that it has been established with me that it came down in the position of spoils, a fifth taken from it. Umar wrote to him to take from it the fifth, and pay what remains of it after the fifth to whoever found it. From Mamar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl: That Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz took from amber the fifth."
"6803","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked: Is there sadaqah in the silent wealth of the orphan? He was surprised and said: Why should there be no sadaqah upon it? He said: Yes, upon the silent wealth of the orphan, and crops, and livestock, and other than that of his wealth."
"6804","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say concerning one who manages the wealth of the orphan? Jabir said: He pays its zakat."
"6805","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yusuf ibn Mahak said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek growth in the wealth of the orphan, lest zakat consume it."
"6806","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad say: Aishah used to send our wealth by sea for trade, and she used to pay its zakat."
"6807","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: We were orphans in the care of Aishah, and she used to pay zakat on our wealth; then she handed it over as mudarabah, and we were blessed in it."
"6808","From al-Thawri, from Layth, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, and Muslim ibn Kathir, all of them from al-Qasim, he said: Our wealth was with Aishah, and she used to pay its zakat, while we were orphans."
"6809","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, he said: Ali sold for us land for eighty thousand. When we wanted to take possession of our wealth, it was deficient. He said: I used to pay its zakat, while we were orphans in his care."
"6810","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to pay zakat on the wealth of an orphan. He said to Uthman ibn Abi al-As: I have wealth of an orphan with me in which zakat has been hastened; are there traders with you to whom I may hand it over? He said: So he handed over to him ten thousand, and he went with it, having a boy. When the year came, he came to Umar, and Umar said to him: What happened to the wealth of the orphan? He said: I have brought it to you. He said: Was there profit in it? He said: Yes, it reached one hundred thousand. He said: And how did you do it? He said: I handed it to the traders, and informed them of the standing of the orphan with you. Umar said: None before you was more deserving in our view not to feed us unlawful than you. Return our principal, and we have no need of your profit."
"6811","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi Umayyah, and Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Humayd ibn Hilal, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to Uthman ibn Abi al-As: We have with us wealth of orphans, fearing that sadaqah will consume it; so take it and work with it. He went out and profited with it eighty thousand. He said: Umar said: A fine pearl used to pass by you, and you would say: This is for the Commander of the Faithful. Return to us our principals."
"6812","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rafi, from Mujahid, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Trade with the wealth of orphans, and pay its sadaqah."
"6813","From al-Thawri, from Thawr, from Abu Awn, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Seek growth in the wealth of orphans before zakat consumes it."
"6814","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar used to pay zakat on the wealth of the orphan."
"6815","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that he used to pay zakat on the wealth of the orphan."
"6816","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that Umar said: Seek growth for the orphans in their wealth."
"6817","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying about the zakat of the orphan's wealth: There is no zakat on him just as there is no prayer on him."
"6818","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: I asked him about the orphan's wealth, and he said: I have wealth of my brother's son with me, and I do not pay zakat on it."
"6819","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, and from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no zakat on the orphan's wealth."
"6820","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: He was asked about the wealth of orphans, and he said: When they come of age, inform them of what zakat has become due on it; if they wish they pay it, and if they wish they leave it."
"6821","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar would have the orphan's wealth with him, and he would borrow it to protect it from loss, and he would pay its zakat from their wealth, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise, except that he said: Then he would pay its zakat every year from their wealth."
"6822","From Ma'mar, he said: Al-Zuhri was asked about the orphan's wealth, how it should be handled, he said: All of that was done; among them were those who would borrow it and protect it from loss, and among them were those who would say: It is only a trust, so I will not leave it until I hand it to its owner, and among them were those who would take it as a profit-sharing partnership, and all of that depends on the intention."
"6823","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar would have the wealth of orphans with him, and he would borrow their wealth to protect it from loss, and he would pay its zakat every year from their wealth."
"6824","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: There is no sadaqa on a male slave, nor a female slave, nor a mukatab. He said: It has reached us that he said: A slave is not entered in the register, and no zakat is taken from him."
"6825","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah Abu Hashim, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: There is no zakat on a debt, nor on a slave, nor on a mukatab, nor on one who buys on credit."
"6826","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed us that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: There is no sadaqa on the wealth of the slave, nor the mukatab, until they are freed."
"6827","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no sadaqa on a slave for his wealth, nor on a master for his slave's wealth."
"6828","Ma'mar said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning the mukatab that no sadaqa is taken from him."
"6829","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: There is no sadaqa on the slave for his wealth."
"6830","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Khalid al-Hadhha, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about the sadaqa on the slave's wealth, and he said: Is he not a Muslim? I said: Yes. He said: Then on him for every two hundred dirhams is five dirhams, and whatever is more is calculated accordingly."
"6831","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Hujayr informed me that Tawus used to say: There is zakat on the slave's wealth."
"6832","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: There is no zakat on the mukatab's wealth."
"6833","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, from his father, he said: My mother passed with her cows by Masruq while she was a mukatabah, and he did not take anything from her. He said: And he was in charge of al-Silsilah."
"6834","From al-Thawri, from Abu Jahm, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked him while I was a mukatab: Is zakat due on me? He said: No."
"6835","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I reviewed with Ata about my slave's wealth, and I said that it is deposited with me, I knew that it is cash, no one has a debt on it, and he does not trade in anything, nor mix with people. He said: You knew that no one has anything on it? He said: And there is no zakat in it. He said: It is said: A slave is not entered in the register, and no zakat is taken from him. I said: The zakat of wealth? He said: Yes."
"6836","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: There is no sadaqa for the slave from his own wealth except with his master's permission."
"6837","From Ibn Jurayj, Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: There is no sadaqa for a slave without his master's permission."
"6838","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi narrating that Abdullah ibn Umar says: It is not allowed for the slave to give anyone anything from his wealth, nor to free, nor to give charity from it, except with his master's permission; but he eats appropriately, and he and his child and his wife clothe themselves."
"6839","From Malik, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to say: Nothing is lawful for the slave from his master's wealth except to eat, or to clothe himself, or to spend appropriately."
"6840","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: There is no sadaqa for the slave without his master's permission."
"6841","From al-Thawri, from al-Ajlah, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: I was with Abdullah ibn Abbas when a man came and said: I am a slave, and a passerby passes me and asks for milk to drink, so may I give him to drink? He said: No. He said: What if I fear he will die of thirst? He said: Give him to drink what will keep him alive apart from you, then ask your family for permission for what you gave him."
"6842","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I asked Amir al-Sha'bi about the slave, does he have sadaqa? He said: No, and his testimony is not valid."
"6843","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The slave may give in charity an insignificant thing."
"6844","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, he said: Dirham informed me that he complained to Abu Hurayrah about his masters, and asked him whether he may give charity. Abu Hurayrah said to him: It is not lawful for you from your wealth except to eat appropriately or to hand a poor person a morsel in his hand."
"6845","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdullah about the sadaqa of the slave, and he said: Let him do what good he can. And Abd al-Wahhab mentioned, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, likewise."
"6846","From Ma'mar, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: Whoever acquires wealth, there is no zakat on it until a year passes over it."
"6847","From Malik, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, that he asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about a mukatab of his who settled with him for much wealth, whether there is zakat on what he took from him. Al-Qasim said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq would not take zakat from wealth until a year passed over it, and when he gave a man a stipend he would ask him: Do you have wealth on which zakat is due? If he said yes, he took from his stipend the zakat of that wealth, and otherwise he handed him his stipend in full. From al-Thawri, and Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from his brother, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad likewise."
"6848","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: On newly acquired wealth, when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas likewise."
"6849","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who has wealth with a month or two left before he pays its zakat, then he wants to spend it. He said: The Muslims used to like that a man pay his zakat before he spends it."
"6850","From Malik, from Umar ibn Husayn, from A'ishah bint Qudamah, from her father, he said: When I received my stipend from Uthman, he would say: Do you have wealth on which zakat is due? If I said yes, he took from my stipend the zakat of that wealth, and otherwise he handed me my stipend."
"6851","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever acquires wealth, there is no zakat on him until a year passes over it. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"6852","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Wealth would come to him and he would not pay zakat on it until a year passed over it even if he spent it all, and he would spend it on rightful need, and he used to say: There is no sadaqa on wealth until a year passes over it; when a year passes over it, then on every two hundred dirhams five dirhams are due, and whatever is more is calculated accordingly."
"6853","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: There is no sadaqa on wealth until a year passes over it. I said: And it cannot be for more than a year? He said: No. I said to him: Its sadaqa went away, then it stayed with me eleven months, then it occurred to me to sell it, is sadaqa due on me for it? He said: No, and that you give it in charity is greater for blessing. I said: Livestock stayed with me eleven months, then I sold it, and the collector came out after it had stayed with him a month, on which of us is the sadaqa? He said: On the one who bought it."
"6854","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died, wealth came to Abu Bakr from Ibn al-Hadrami, and Abu Bakr said: Whoever had a debt on the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or had a promise from him, let him come to us. Jabir said: So I said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, promised to give me thus and thus, and he spread his hands three times. Jabir said: So he counted into my hand five hundred, then five hundred, then five hundred. And another besides him added: That he said to Jabir: There is no sadaqa on you for it until a year passes over you for it."
"6855","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: On newly acquired wealth, when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due on it."
"6856","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: He would give, then he would take its zakat."
"6857","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Barqan, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when he gave a man his stipend or his wage, would take zakat from him."
"6858","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Hujayr informed me, from Tawus, he said: If you put wealth before the year into something you do not trade, there is no sadaqa on it."
"6859","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said, and he was asked while I was listening, about a man given a prize, should he pay zakat on it at once or until a year passes? He said: More beloved to me and greater for its blessing is that he pay zakat on it at once, but if he delays it to the year, there is no harm."
"6860","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan, he said: If you have wealth on which you want to pay zakat with a month or two left before the year, then you acquire wealth, then pay zakat on it with it; pay zakat on both together."
"6861","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whoever acquires wealth pays its zakat along with his wealth."
"6862","From al-Thawri, he said: And it is said: If one acquires wealth after zakat became due on his wealth, even if he has not yet paid it, the one he acquired starts a new year."
"6863","Sufyan said: If a man has wealth amounting to zakat, then that wealth of his goes away and one dirham remains of it, with a month left before the time he used to pay zakat in, then he acquires wealth, he pays zakat on the wealth he acquired along with that dirham; but if the wealth is gone and nothing remains of it, he does not pay zakat on what he acquired until the new year he started with it."
"6864","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: No sadaqa is taken from profits if the principal wealth was already zakated, until a year passes over it."
"6865","From al-Thawri, about a man who acquires wealth and it stays until only one day remains before zakat becomes due on it, then he gains a thousand, he said: He pays zakat on both together. And if he has wealth he used to pay zakat on, and all but one dirham goes away, then he gains wealth a month or two or less before his zakat time, then that dirham is stolen, he said: He pays zakat on the wealth he acquired because he had gained the wealth while the dirham was in his possession. Sufyan said: If one buys cloth for two hundred and it grows by the year until it reaches a thousand, he pays zakat on the thousand; but if it decreases after reaching a thousand to a hundred, he does not pay zakat on it. Sufyan said about a man who buys an animal or goods for trade for one hundred and ninety, then it grows until its value reaches a thousand or more, he said: There is no zakat on it until he converts it into something else, because the price he bought it with had no zakat on it; when he converts it into something else, he does not pay zakat on it until a year passes over it. And if he buys it for two hundred and it reaches ten dirhams, there is no zakat on him for it; but if he buys it for two hundred and it reaches a thousand, then zakat of the thousand is on him, because the principal had zakat on it. He said: If a man buys goods for trade then decides to keep them afterward, he has cancelled the trade; but if he decides to put them into trade, there is no zakat on them until he converts them. Sufyan said about a man owed two hundred dirhams by a man, and he repays him one hundred dirhams: There is no zakat on him for it until he takes the other, unless he has wealth, in which case he adds it to his wealth and pays zakat on it; but if he takes the two hundred and has no other wealth besides them, he pays zakat on the two hundred once, because when he takes five dirhams from it, the remainder does not have what zakat is due on. He said: And whoever has cloth and values it at a thousand dirhams, then does not pay zakat on it until it decreases to five hundred dirhams, then zakat of the thousand is on him; but if he valued it at five hundred then left it until it reached a thousand, then only zakat of five hundred is on him; and if he has cloth and values it at a hundred until it reaches a thousand, there is nothing on him."
"6866","From Umar ibn Dharr al-Hamdani, he said: I asked Amir al-Sha'bi about the zakat of jewelry, and he said: Its zakat is lending it. Umar said: And my father advised me to pay zakat on a necklace on my sister's neck. My father said: And it used to be said: A stored thing, when zakated once, is not zakated again until it is turned over into something else."
"6867","From al-Thawri, and Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah about jewelry, whether there is zakat on it. He said: No. I said: Even if it is a thousand dinars? He said: A thousand is much."
"6868","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: There is no zakat on jewelry. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard the same from Jabir, like what Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir likewise."
"6869","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: There is no zakat on jewelry, and she is indeed foolish who adorns herself with what zakat is due on."
"6870","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, that she asked A'ishah about jewelry of hers, whether sadaqa is due on her for it. She said: No."
"6871","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, that A'ishah used to adorn her brother's daughters with gold and pearls and would not pay zakat on it, and their jewelry in those days was little."
"6872","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: There is no zakat on jewelry."
"6873","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Zakat on jewelry is every year."
"6874","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: A woman asked him about jewelry of hers, whether there is zakat on it. He said: When it reaches two hundred dirhams, pay zakat on it. She said: I have orphans in my care, may I give it to them? He said: Yes."
"6875","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: The wife of Abdullah said: I have jewelry, should I pay zakat on it? He said: When it reaches two hundred dirhams, pay zakat on it. She said: My brother's orphaned sons are in my care, may I place it among them? He said: Yes."
"6876","From al-Thawri, from Abu Musa, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that he used to adorn his daughters with gold, he mentioned more than two hundred dirhams, I think he mentioned a thousand or more, and he would pay zakat on it."
"6877","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ja'far, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, he said: Zakat is on jewelry, even on a ring."
"6878","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Zakat is on gold and silver jewelry."
"6879","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr informed me that he asked Ibn al-Musayyib: Is there zakat on gold and silver jewelry? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Then it will perish. He said: Even so."
"6880","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Sadaqa is on gold ore and silver ore, whether it is traded or not traded, whether cast and stored, and whether in a woman's jewelry. He said: And there is no sadaqa on pearls, nor chrysolite, nor ruby, nor stone settings, nor goods not traded; but if any of that is traded, then sadaqa is on its price when it is sold."
"6881","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no zakat on jewels and ruby, unless it is for trade."
"6882","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Gold and silver jewelry is zakated, and there is no zakat on beads unless it is for trade."
"6883","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no zakat on ruby and its likes, unless some of it is traded."
"6884","From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that two Yemeni women came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he saw gold rings on their hands, and he said: Do you pay its zakat? They said: No. He said: Would it please you that Allah ring you on the Day of Resurrection with rings of fire? Or he said: Would it please you that He bracelet you on the Day of Resurrection with two bracelets of fire? They said: No. He said: Then pay its zakat."
"6885","From al-Thawri, he said: We say: The ornament of a sword, and a belt, and all gold and silver, you join it with your wealth when it pays zakat, you pay zakat on it. And if foods of each type are a wasq or two wasqs, nothing is due on them until one type completes five wasqs, except that gold and silver have a rule unlike others: if there are ten mithqals of gold and a hundred dirhams, he pays zakat on it."
"6886","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yazid Abu Khalid informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to al-Abbas at the time of zakat: Pay the zakat of your wealth, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered that. Al-Abbas said: I paid it before that. Umar mentioned that to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: He spoke the truth; he paid it beforehand."
"6887","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked him about a man who sees the place for his zakat and hastens it. He said: There is no harm in hastening it."
"6888","From al-Thawri, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in hastening it."
"6889","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, he was asked about that, and he said: And why should he hasten his zakat? As if he disliked that."
"6890","From Ma'mar, from Hafs, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no harm in hastening it. Ma'mar said: And Ibn Sirin disliked that."
"6891","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: Anas ibn Malik sent me over al-Aylah. He said: I said: You sent me over your worst work. He said: So he brought out for me a letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab: Take from the Muslims, from every forty dirhams a dirham, and from the people of the covenant, from every twenty dirhams a dirham, and from one who has no covenant, from every ten dirhams a dirham. From al-Thawri, and Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Anas likewise."
"6892","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: On two hundred dirhams five dirhams are due, and whatever is more is calculated accordingly. He said: I said: What is his saying: Whatever is more is calculated accordingly? He said: Some of them say: When it increases over two hundred and the increase is forty dirhams, then a dirham is due on it. And others said: Whatever is more is calculated accordingly: when it is ten, then a quarter dirham is due on it."
"6893","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whatever is more than two hundred is calculated accordingly."
"6894","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, he said: Ali said: Whoever acquires wealth, there is no zakat on him until a year passes over it; when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due on it, and if it is less than two hundred, there is nothing on it, and if it is more than two hundred, then proportionally."
"6895","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Ali, I have exempted the sadaqa of horses and slaves; as for camels, cattle, and sheep, no; but bring a quarter of a tenth: from every two hundred dirhams five dirhams, and from every twenty dinars half a dinar. And there is nothing on two hundred dirhams until a year passes over them; when a year passes over them, five dirhams are due on them, and whatever is more, then on every forty dirhams a dirham is due."
"6896","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: Whatever is more than two hundred, nothing is taken from it until it reaches forty. And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Ata, and from Hisham ibn Hujayr, from Tawus likewise, and Hisham, from al-Hasan likewise."
"6897","From Hisham, from Muhammad, from Khalid al-Hadhha, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whatever is more than two hundred is by calculation."
"6898","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever is more than two hundred is by calculation."
"6899","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, about a man who has a hundred dirhams and ten dinars, they said: Sadaqa is due on him for the dinars and dirhams. Al-Thawri said: He joins the lesser to the greater. And Waki said: And Ibn Abi Layla used to say: There is nothing on them, like cattle and sheep, until the dirhams reach two hundred dirhams."
"6900","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata and Amr ibn Dinar said: There is no sadaqa on wealth until it reaches twenty dinars; when it reaches twenty dinars, half a dinar is due on it, then for every four dinars the wealth increases, a dirham is due, until the wealth reaches forty dinars, then on every forty dinars a dinar is due. He said: And on twenty-four dinars, half a dinar and a dirham are due. I said: On twenty dinars, half a dinar in full? He said: Yes, until after that by a while. I said to him: If a man has nineteen dinars and nothing else, and the exchange is twelve or thirteen for a dinar, is there sadaqa on them? He said: Yes, if when exchanged they reach two hundred dirhams; for then it was silver and there was no gold. He said: And there is no sadaqa on silver until it reaches two hundred dirhams; when it reaches two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due on it, then for every forty dirhams the wealth increases, a dirham is due. And Amr ibn Dinar said that. He said: And Ata said: Until the wealth reaches four hundred dirhams, then on every four hundred dirhams ten dirhams are due. I said: Two hundred dirhams and twenty dirhams? He said: There is nothing on twenty dirhams. And Amr ibn Dinar said it to me."
"6901","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: Until it reaches forty dirhams extra, then it is six at that point, then nothing until it reaches two hundred and eighty, then it is seven, and so on. Ata said: And if they are twenty-three dinars, then on the twenty is half a dinar, and if the exchange reaches forty-three dirhams, then a dirham is due on them, otherwise not. He said: And Abd al-Karim said to me the like of Ata's saying: On forty and some extra a dirham is due, and there is nothing on less than forty extra."
"6902","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Hujayr informed me, from Tawus, that it used to be said: On two hundred dirhams five dirhams are due, and there is nothing on anything after two hundred until it reaches forty dirhams extra."
"6903","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is nothing on less than two hundred dirhams; when they reach two hundred dirhams, five dirhams are due on them. He said: And in the letter of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to Amr ibn Hazm: On one's silver, when it reaches five uqiyas, a quarter of a tenth is due."
"6904","From al-Zuhri, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, he said: I heard Uthman preaching, and he was saying: This is your zakat month; whoever owes a debt, let him pay it, then let him pay zakat on what remains."
"6905","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informing us, while we were with Ata, that Uthman, when the stipend came out, would preach and say: Whoever owes a debt, let him settle it, then let him pay zakat on his wealth. Ata said to me at that: By my life, there is no sadaqa on a man's wealth while a debt is on him. Ata said: When they pay zakat on a man's stipend after his debt, the master of the stipend has not wronged. I said to him: What if I owe a debt, and I have wealth, and I have slaves who cover the debt for me, do I pay zakat for myself? He said: Yes."
"6906","From al-Thawri, he said: When your date-palms or your crops are present, look at what debt you owe, old or new, deduct it, then pay zakat on what remains if it reaches five wasqs."
"6907","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man's crop whose debt is greater than his wealth is harvested, should he pay its due on harvest day? He said: I do not see sadaqa on a man whose debt is greater than his wealth, neither on livestock, nor land, nor that he pay its due on harvest day."
"6908","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said to me: I heard Tawus saying: There is no sadaqa on him."
"6909","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Sadaqa is only on what you store after you eat from it, and after you pay the wage, or spend on threshing and other things, until you store it in your house, unless you sell something, then sadaqa is on what you sold."
"6910","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, he said: What you give from your food in your spending is counted in the food, and what you eat too, except something you measure out for your family. He says: You measure it for them."
"6911","From Sufyan, about a hunter who keeps his game a year, or keeps birds a year: There is no zakat on it until he keeps it in something he trades for commerce. Sufyan said: And everyone who inherits something, there is no zakat on him until he converts it, except a man who inherits cattle, or sheep, or camels, or gold, or silver, or crops."
"6912","From Ma'mar, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from al-Nakha'i, he said: Whoever has goods for trade and they stay with him years without selling them, zakat is on them every year, he pays its zakat. He said: And al-Sha'bi said: There is no zakat on it after the first time."
"6913","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: Food from his land would be with my father, and it would stay with him two and three years while he wanted to sell it, and he would not pay zakat on it after the first zakat, waiting for high prices. Abd al-Razzaq said: A name I do not like to mention, waiting for high prices."
"6914","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: If I had sheep and I paid zakat on them, then I sold from their wool and milk for two hundred dirhams, there would be no zakat on the two hundred until a year passes over it, if the sheep themselves had been zakated. He said: And al-Hakam ibn Utaybah said that."
"6915","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Ju'fi about a man who has food from his land he wants to sell, whose original was zakated. Al-Sha'bi said: There is no zakat on it until it is sold. And al-Nakha'i said: There is zakat on it. From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, like al-Sha'bi's saying. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: We have heard nothing about it besides the first. Ibn Jurayj said: And Abd al-Karim said it."
"6916","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Salamah, from Hamas, he said: Umar passed by me and said: Pay the zakat of your wealth. He said: So I said: I have no wealth to pay zakat on except boots and hides. He said: So evaluate it and pay its zakat."
"6917","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said about cloth: If it is traded like slaves, he pays zakat on its price."
"6918","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, about a man who has various grains on none of which zakat is due, he said: He combines them then pays zakat on them."
"6919","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to say: There is no zakat on goods not traded except gold and silver; for if it is stored ingots, even if not traded, it is zakated."
"6920","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whatever wealth was in slaves, or in animals, or in cloth traded for commerce, zakat is due on it every year."
"6921","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, and from Abu al-Zinad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that they said: On goods traded, zakat is due every year; zakat is not taken from them until that month comes in the next year. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard Ibn Abi Sabrah myself saying: Amr ibn Sulaym and Abu al-Nadr informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, and Abu al-Zinad, from Urwah likewise."
"6922","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard myself that it is the value of the goods on the day its zakat is paid."
"6923","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The advance a man advances? He said: There is no sadaqa on the owner of the wealth, nor on the one he advanced it to, and it is then like a debt regarding sadaqa, except that it is greater in reward than a debt. He, they claim, is the one who says: The gold loan is the advance. He is the speaker."
"6924","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that a man informed him: An orphan had wealth with Ibn Umar, and it was said: Pay zakat on it. Ibn Umar said: Soon."
"6925","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar borrowed an orphan's wealth, and it stayed with him three years, and he would pay zakat on it during those three years, paying it from their wealth."
"6926","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar would have the wealth of orphans with him, and he would borrow their wealth to protect it from loss, then he would pay its zakat from their wealth every year. From al-Thawri, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"6927","From Mughirah, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: If your debt is on a trustworthy person, pay zakat on it; but if you fear loss on it, do not pay zakat on it until you receive it. From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"6928","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Tawus, he said: On every goods, and cash debt hoped for, zakat is due."
"6929","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ata, he said: There is no zakat on a debt."
"6930","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah, she said: There is no zakat on a debt."
"6931","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad, from Ubaydah, from Ali, he said: He was asked about a man who has a debt on a man. He said: What prevents him from paying zakat on it? He said: He cannot get it. He said: Even if he is truthful, let him pay for what was absent from him. From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Muhammad, from Shurayh, from Ali likewise. From Hisham, from Muhammad, from Shurayh likewise."
"6932","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi ibn al-Khawzi informed me, he said: I was sitting with Abd al-Rahman ibn Nafi when Ziyad the doorkeeper came to him and said: The Commander of the Faithful, Ibn al-Zubayr, says: Send the zakat of your wealth. He said: Did he send you? He said: Yes. And he did not review him with other than it until he stood, then he brought out a hundred dirhams. He said: So convey peace to him, and say: Zakat is only from cash. Nafi said: So I met Ziyad afterward and said: Did you convey what he said? He said: Yes. I said: So what did he say? He said: He spoke the truth. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ubaydullah Abu Yazid narrated to me similar to that, from Ziyad."
"6933","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said: I do not see sadaqa except on cash."
"6934","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: On a debt a man has on another, should he pay its zakat? He said: Yes."
"6935","Ibn Jurayj said: Ata used to see no sadaqa on a debt, even if years passed, until when it came out he would pay zakat on it once. And he used to say about a man who buys with wealth and it becomes due, then when it becomes due he buys with it and transfers it to his creditors without receiving it, he said: There is no sadaqa on it. Ata said: Even if it is secured, he does not pay zakat on it until it comes out."
"6936","He said: And Abd al-Karim said: He pays zakat on the debt every year until it becomes due if it is secured. I said: Wealth I secured and it was stolen from me, or from the money-changer, or the money-changer went bankrupt, he said: There is nothing on it. He said: It stayed with me a month or more, then it was stolen or met destruction, whatever it was, then there is no zakat on it if you intended to pay zakat on it. He said: What if I had slaves I hire out year to year for four hundred dinars, he said: So he hastened me. He said: And when you take the wealth, pay zakat on it. He said: I knew, but do I pay zakat for them on the day of breaking fast? He said: Yes, and that is because you advance from the wealth, and some of the lads complain and run away. I said to him: If the sadaqa of my wealth becomes due while I am in a land other than my land, do I pay my sadaqa to the collector of that land, or delay it until I pay it to the collector of my land? He said: It is the same; it does not harm you to whichever of them you pay it."
"6937","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir informed me that Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr informed him that a man said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: O Commander of the Faithful, we have expenses with us, so I hasten the sadaqa, and I spend on my family, and I settle my debt. He said: So do not hasten with it; when it comes, count your debt, what is on you, gather all that together, then pay zakat on it."
"6938","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, he said: A man said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: My zakat time comes and I owe a debt? So he ordered him to pay zakat on it."
"6939","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: There is no zakat on a debt."
"6940","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: There is no zakat on a debt."
"6941","From al-Thawri, about a man whose enemy seized a thousand dirhams from him and he recovered them after a year, he said: There is no zakat on him for them until a year passes from the day he took them, because they were consumed; had the Muslims seized them, they would have divided them."
"6942","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: Urwah ibn Muhammad wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about wealth in which people were wronged, and it was in the hands of officials. So he wrote: Return it to them, and take zakat from them on it. His official reviewed with him about that: Should he take it from every year or one year? So he wrote to him: If it is lost wealth, then pay zakat on it for one year. I said to him: What is lost wealth? He said: What is gone."
"6943","From Ma'mar, he said: I said to Qatadah: Absent wealth, is there zakat on it? He said: If it is not lost or destroyed, pay zakat on it."
"6944","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: Zakat is on the one in whose hand the wealth is. He said: And Ibn al-Musayyib used to say: If the debt and the advance are on a solvent person, then its owner must pay its zakat; but if they are on an insolvent person, there is no zakat on them until they come out, then zakat of the past years is due on them. He said: That is the rule."
"6945","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: There is no zakat on a debt until it is received; when it is received, he pays zakat on it once."
"6946","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who has a debt, should he pay zakat on it? He said: Yes, if it is on a trustworthy person; but if he fears loss on it, he does not pay zakat on it, and when he receives it, he pays zakat on it for what was absent from him. From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim likewise."
"6947","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that he used to take goods in their zakat from the people of Yemen."
"6948","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from a man who narrated to him, from Umar, that he used to take goods in zakat, and he would put them in one category of people."
"6949","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Sadaqat are only for the poor, and I recited the verse to him. I said: Are all the sadaqat for them? He said: Yes, if you place your zakat in one category, or two categories, or three, even if it is much, I ordered him to place it in all of them."
"6950","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If you place it in one category of these categories, it suffices you."
"6951","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was reported to me, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: If you place it in one category of these categories, it suffices you. Allah only said: Sadaqat are only for the poor, and so and so, so that you do not place it outside these categories."
"6952","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, he said: Each worker is given according to his work. And al-Thawri said: The worker gets what suffices him of spending and clothing, and he is the one who undertakes receiving the sadaqa."
"6953","From Abd al-Samad ibn Mi'qal, he said: I heard Wahb saying: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Urwah ibn Muhammad: Do not divide the sadaqa into eighths, and give each worker according to him, and the poor and needy according to their need and disability. Abd al-Samad said: And Amr ibn Abi Yazid informed me: That he came asking her scholars, man by man, and they said: That is only the opinion of the imam and his judgment; if he sees to prefer some over some, he prefers, and if he sees to divide it into parts, he does."
"6954","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you pay the sadaqa of your wealth, it is not a hoard, even if buried; but if you do not pay it, it is a hoard, even if apparent."
"6955","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whatever its zakat is paid is not a hoard, even if under seven earths; and whatever is apparent whose zakat is not paid is a hoard. From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar likewise."
"6956","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: When you pay the zakat of your wealth, it is not a hoard, even if buried; but if you do not pay its zakat, it is a hoard, even if apparent. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One who heard Nafi informed me, mentioning from Ibn Umar likewise, and he added: The hoard Allah mentioned in His Book is only what its zakat is not paid on."
"6957","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: When you pay the sadaqa of your wealth, you have removed its evil, and it is not a hoard."
"6958","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, that a man sold a man a walled garden of his or wealth for great wealth, and Umar ibn al-Khattab said to him: Put this wealth in a good place. The man said to him: Where shall I put it, O Commander of the Faithful? Umar said: Put it under the woman's seat. The man said: Is it not a hoard, O Commander of the Faithful? Umar said: It is not a hoard when you pay its zakat. He said: And Ziyad informed me, he said: He is only Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, then he informed him with a similar story."
"6959","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: It used to be said: Zakat is a bridge between the Fire and Paradise; whoever pays his zakat crosses the bridge."
"6960","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from two men between him and Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Whoever earns lawful wealth, withholding zakat corrupts it; and whoever earns unlawful wealth, zakat does not purify it."
"6961","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Zurayq ibn Abi Sulaym, from Yazid al-Raqashi, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: There is no prayer except with zakat."
"6962","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Abu al-Duha, from Ja'dah ibn Hubayrah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, who said: Four thousand dirhams and less is maintenance, and what is above it is hoarding."
"6963","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for a rich man except for five: one employed in collecting it, or a man who bought it with his money, or a debtor, or a warrior in the path of Allah, or a poor man to whom charity was given from it and he gifted from it to a rich man. From al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6964","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from Ibrahim, who said: They would not ask except about one in need."
"6965","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was distributing on the day of the Conquest, and two men came and asked him, so he looked them up and down, and Ma'mar said, meaning two sturdy men, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: As you wish, but there is no share in it for a rich man nor for a strong able earner."
"6966","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Rayhan ibn Yazid al-Amiri, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is not lawful for a rich man nor for one possessing full strength."
"6967","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: A man from Banu Layth called Kardam informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to them: Give from charity to one whom the drought year has left with sheep and their shepherd, and do not give from it to one whom the drought year has left with two flocks and two shepherds."
"6968","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said: One who has fifty dirhams is not given from charity, and no one is given from it more than fifty dirhams, unless he is a debtor burdened with debt."
"6969","From al-Thawri, who said: Ibrahim al-Nakha'i said: Whoever has fifty dirhams does not take from charity unless he is a debtor."
"6970","From al-Thawri, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: He gives from charity one hundred up to two hundred. Sufyan said: And it reached me the like of it from al-Sha'bi."
"6971","From Ma'mar, who said: One who heard Ikrimah informed me, he says: Give the zakat of your wealth to your near relatives; if there are none, then to your freed slaves; if there are none, then to your neighbors."
"6972","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: Give it to your near relatives among whom you live; if you find none, then to those next to them. Sufyan said: And some of our jurists used to prefer relatives; if there are none, then freed slaves; if there are none, then neighbors; and he does not take it out of that town."
"6973","From al-Thawri, from one who heard al-Hasan say: When a man has nothing but a house and a servant, he takes zakat. He said: And our companions say that, and al-Hasan did not consider Hajj obligatory on one who has nothing but a house and a servant."
"6974","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: There is no harm in placing your zakat in its proper place, so long as you do not give from it to anyone whose maintenance you bear; there is no harm in it."
"6975","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Hafsah, who said: I said to Sa'id ibn Jubayr: May I give my maternal aunt from zakat? He said: Yes, so long as you have not shut a door upon her, meaning so long as she is not among your dependents."
"6976","From al-Thawri, from Amr, and al-Rabi', from al-Hasan, that he used to prefer to divide equally between his relatives and others in zakat, saying: When he gives them."
"6977","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, who said: A Jew and a Christian are not given from zakat; they are given from voluntary charity."
"6978","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan, who said: Neither a slave nor an idolater is given from zakat."
"6979","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, that Amr ibn Shurahbil used to give zakat al-fitr to monks among the protected non-Muslims, while another used to say: He gives it to Muslims."
"6980","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq, who said: Amr ibn Shurahbil used to collect zakat al-fitr in the mosque of his quarter, then distribute it among monks."
"6981","From al-Thawri, who said: A man does not give the zakat of his wealth to one of his relatives whose maintenance is obligatory upon him, nor gives it for the shroud of a dead person, nor for the debt of a dead person, nor for building a mosque, nor for buying a mushaf, nor performs Hajj with it, nor gives it to his mukatab, nor buys with it a slave to set him free, nor gives it among the Jews nor the Christians, nor hires from it one to carry it from place to place."
"6982","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd, who said: I said to Ibrahim: May I give my sister from my zakat? He said: Yes."
"6983","From al-Thawri, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prescribe zakat on anything except ten things: gold, silver, cattle, sheep, camels, wheat, barley, raisins, sorghum, and dates."
"6984","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: There is no charity except on date-palms, grapes, or tilled crops; and Amr ibn Dinar and Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq said that."
"6985","I said to Ata: Is charity due on all grain? He said: Yes, and he named all grain for me. He said: I said: In sorghum, millet, vetches, lentils, and safflower seed? He said: Yes, in all grain. He said: I said: Taqdidah? He said: There is charity in it; it is grain; charity is in all grain. I said: Then is there no charity in anything besides that? He said: No, meaning by taqdidah: coriander."
"6986","Ata said: If the dates of palms and the fruit of grapes are sold for gold, and the ruler accepts the sale by the owner of the wealth concerning the wealth and does not assess it against him, then he owes only one dinar in every forty dinars. I said to him: Is there charity on grain carried by sea, already taxed when harvested, being circulating wealth - is the gold taxed when it returns? He said: No; if it was taxed once that suffices it. But if it yields gold after a year, he taxes it also. And I say concerning the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), In what the sky waters: it is an explanation of the charity on grain."
"6987","Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: There is no zakat on utb and wars. From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Ikrimah the like of it."
"6988","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a piece of silver and said: O Messenger of Allah, take its zakat from it. He said: From where is it? He said: It is from the mine of such-and-such family. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather we shall give you the like of it, and we shall not return to it."
"6989","From Ma'mar, from a man who used to work in the mines in the time of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar, who said: They used to take from us for what we treat and work with our hands, from every two hundred dirhams five dirhams; but when we found rikaz in the mines, he took the fifth from us."
"6990","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: Whatever is found of spoils, in it is the fifth."
"6991","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali ibn Abi Talib to a rikaz in Yemen and he took its fifth."
"6992","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard that when a man buys land or a house and finds therein ancient buried wealth, it is his, and it is spoils; and if he finds wealth belonging to this ummah, it is his, unless the previous owner brings proof and a known sign."
"6993","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: I was informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The well is jubar, the mine is jubar, the animal is jubar, and in rikaz is the fifth. Jubar is void; and rikaz is what is found of minerals, and what is extracted from it of buried wealth, and something belonging to a generation before this ummah. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: it is spoils."
"6994","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu Sa'id al-A'ma informed me alone, and he informed us along with Ata, who said: Abu Hakim set out to Marwan with the zakat of his wealth, and Marwan said to him: Are you on stipend? He said: No. He said: Then take your zakat back, for we shall not take it from you. He said: Then Marwan assigned him a stipend the next day."
"6995","Then Abu Sa'id said: And Abu Hurayrah met a man carrying the zakat of his wealth, intending the ruler, and Abu Hurayrah said: What is with you? He said: The zakat of my wealth; I am taking it to the ruler. He said to him: Are you on the register? He said: No. He said: Then do not give them anything."
"6996","Then Ata informed me at that time, he said: That reached us from Ali: that a man came to him with the zakat of his wealth, and he said: Do you take from our stipend? He said: No. He said: Then go, for we shall not take from you; we shall not combine against you: we withhold from you and take from you. He said: I said: They say: Zakat is not obligatory on one who has no register. He said: Their zakat is obligatory on them, but they say: We neither take from you nor give you; so he takes and gives them their zakat, because he gives them nothing of the wealth. I said to him: A man has provision in wheat but has nothing in silver. He said: That stipend suffices him. He said: His zakat is then taken from him."
"6997","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'bad ibn Umayr, that people came to Ali with their charities, and he said: Do you take from us? They said: No, and he refused to take from them. Ma'mar said: He only means: We do not take from you, but place it yourselves in its proper places."
"6998","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said: There is no charity on greens, reeds, arugula, cucumbers, cotton, safflower, fruits, citron, apples, walnuts, figs, pomegranates, peaches, and fruits - he counted them all - there is no charity on them, they are only eaten, unless something of them is sold for gold reaching an amount in which there is charity; if something of them is sold for gold reaching such an amount, then in it is the like of the charity on gold. And Abd al-Karim and Amr ibn Dinar said that to me. He said: And Ata said to me concerning the price of fruits and vegetables: when something of them is sold for gold, he said: The gold is then taxed as circulating gold is taxed."
"6999","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib and others, from Musa ibn Talhah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity on vegetables."
"7000","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Uthman, from Musa ibn Talhah, who said: I heard him say: al-Hajjaj sent Musa ibn al-Mughirah over the Sawad, and he wanted to take from the vegetables of the Sawad, so Musa ibn Talhah said: I have the letter of Mu'adh ibn Jabal from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he ordered him to take from wheat, barley, raisins, and dates. He said: So I mentioned that to al-Hajjaj, and he said: He spoke the truth."
"7001","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Mawhib, who said: I heard Ibn Talhah, meaning Musa - and they had taken from grains of his on his land - and I heard him say to Abd al-Hamid when he entered upon him: Between me and you is the letter of Mu'adh ibn Jabal: he did not take anything from vegetables."
"7002","From Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, who said: There is no charity on vegetables: greens, apples, and cucumbers. From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ali the like of it."
"7003","From al-Thawri, and Hushaym, from al-Ajlah, from al-Sha'bi, from Ali, who said: There is no charity on summer produce, meaning grains, lentils, and the like."
"7004","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab took zakat from pulses; and pulses are lentils, chickpeas, and the like."
"7005","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning vegetables and fruit: when their price reaches two hundred dirhams, in it are five dirhams."
"7006","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning olives, he said: It is measured, so in it is the tenth if not irrigated, and half the tenth if irrigated with a rope-drawn bucket."
"7007","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: There is no zakat on vegetables. He said: So I mentioned it to Ibrahim, and he said: He spoke the truth."
"7008","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: On everything the earth grows is the tenth."
"7009","From Ma'mar, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Take from what the earth grows, little or much, the tenth. From Ma'mar, who said: That reached me from Mujahid."
"7010","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: There is no zakat on utb and wars."
"7011","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, who said: Their assessment was this in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as they claimed."
"7012","From Ma'mar, from Haram ibn Uthman, from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to send a man of the Ansar from Banu Bayadah called Farwah ibn Amr, and he would assess the dates of the people of Madinah. Ma'mar said: And I have not heard of assessment except on date-palms and grapes."
"7013","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Was their assessment this in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He informed me, from Ibn Rawahah: that he assessed between the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Jews, and said: If you wish it is ours, and if you wish it is yours. They said: By this the heavens and the earth stand."
"7014","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: When Ibn Rawahah came to them, they gathered for him jewelry from their women's jewelry and gifted it to him, and he said: O company of Jews, by Allah you are the most hateful of Allah's creation to me, yet that shall not move me to be unjust to you; as for what you have offered me of this bribe, it is illicit gain and we do not eat it. Then he assessed against them, then gave them the choice to take it or to hand it to him at that assessment, and they said: By this the heavens and the earth stand; so they took it at that assessment."
"7015","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no workers to work it on the palms and crops of Khaybar, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the Jews of Khaybar and handed Khaybar to them on condition that they work it for half, paying it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: I settle you in it as long as Allah settles you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send Abdullah ibn Rawahah to them, and he would assess upon them when the first fruit ripened before any of it was eaten, then give the Jews the choice to take it at the assessment or hand it over at that assessment. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only ordered assessment so that zakat would be reckoned before the fruits were eaten and scattered; and they remained upon that."
"7016","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me, concerning the settlement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the Jews of Khaybar, that we have half the fruit and they have half, he said: and they undertake the work; until when their fruit ripened they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Our fruit has ripened, so send an assessor between us and you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ibn Rawahah; when he went round their palms and looked at them he said: By Allah, I know no one among Allah's creation guilty of greater fabrication and more hostile to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than you; by Allah, Allah has created no one more hateful to me than you, yet that shall not move me to wrong you by even a mote's weight that I know of. He said: Then he assessed it all, what was theirs and what was for the Jews, eighty thousand wasqs. Then the Jews said: You have wronged us. Ibn Rawahah said: If you wish, give us forty thousand wasqs and you leave it to us; and if you wish, we give you forty thousand wasqs and you leave it to us. They looked at one another and said: By this the heavens and the earth stand, and by this they shall overcome you."
"7017","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: Ibn Rawahah assessed it at forty thousand wasqs; and he claimed that when Ibn Rawahah gave the Jews the choice, they took the dates, with twenty thousand wasqs due upon them."
"7018","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: It is binding on the assessor, when the owner of the wealth deems the assessment excessive, to give him the choice as Ibn Rawahah gave the choice. He said: Yes, by my life; and what practice is better than the practice of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"7019","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amir ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nistas informed me, concerning Khaybar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered it, and it belonged wholly to him, its tilth and its palms; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions had no slaves, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with the Jews that you undertake the work for us and yours is half the fruit, on condition that I settle you as long as it seems fit to Allah and His Messenger. That is when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ibn Rawahah to assess it between them; when he gave them the choice, the Jews took the fruit. Khaybar remained in Jewish hands under the peace of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Umar came and expelled them. The Jews said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not make peace with us on such-and-such. He said: Yes indeed, on condition that we settle you as long as it seems fit to Allah and His Messenger; and now it seems fit to me to expel you. So he expelled them, then divided it among the Muslims who had conquered it with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), giving none of it to one who was not present at its conquest. He said: So its people now are the Muslims; there are no Jews in it."
"7020","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed Khaybar to the Jews on condition that they work in it and theirs is half of it. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and the early part of Umar's caliphate continued upon that. Then Umar was informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the illness in which he died: Two religions shall not coexist in the land of Hijaz, or in the land of the Arabs. So he investigated that until he found firm proof of it, and said: Whoever has a covenant from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him bring it; otherwise I shall expel you. He said: So he expelled them; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said in the illness in which he was taken."
"7021","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, who said: Ishaq narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Sahl, from Rafi' ibn Khadij: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send Farwah ibn Amr to assess the date-palms; when he entered the garden he counted the bunches in it, then set some against others as he judged, and he would not err."
"7022","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an assessor, he ordered him not to assess the araya."
"7023","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, who said: I heard him say: Assessment today is an innovation. Abd al-Razzaq said: And it reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered assessment upon the Jews once or twice, then left it afterwards."
"7024","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: Date-palms and grapes are assessed, but grain is not assessed. I said to him: Did those of the past assess date-palms and grapes and not assess grain, or is it people today? He said: Rather those of the past did; I think he said: And people today also do not assess."
"7025","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq and Amr ibn Dinar said to me: Date-palms and grapes are assessed, but grain is not assessed."
"7026","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Attab ibn Asid when he appointed him over Makkah, saying: Assess grapes as you assess date-palms, then take its zakat from raisins as you take the zakat of date-palms from dates. Ibn Jurayj said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning the charity on dates: that barni be taken from barni, and lawn be taken from lawn, and lawn not be taken from barni, and that it be taken at the threshing floor and they not combine them. Ibn Jurayj mentioned it from Ibn Abi Najih."
"7027","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: They used to assess the fruit when it ripened and became unripe dates, then leave it to its owners; they would eat it as unripe, ripe, and dried dates, then they would take according to that assessment."
"7028","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the assessment of Khaybar when its fruit ripened."
"7029","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: When are date-palms assessed? He said: When they bear edible fruit. And Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq said so."
"7030","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: And before that, not? He said: Yes, until they bear edible fruit."
"7031","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from A'ishah, that she said, while mentioning the affair of Khaybar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send Ibn Rawahah to the Jews, and he would assess the date-palms when the first fruit ripened, before it was eaten, then give the Jews the choice to take it at that assessment or hand it over at that; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only ordered that assessment so that zakat would be reckoned before the fruits were eaten and scattered."
"7032","From Ma'mar, from Haram ibn Uthman, from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say to the assessors when he sent them: Make allowance for the owners of the wealth for calamity, trespassers, and what is due on the fruit of rightful claims."
"7033","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say to the assessors: Leave for them what falls, and what they eat."
"7034","As for Ma'mar, he narrated to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id that Umar used to say to the assessors: When you find people who have gathered, meaning settled in their garden, then consider what you think they will eat, for it is not assessed against them."
"7035","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What if I assess my date-palms, then sell them after assessment to people, and I establish proof that they are less than assessed - do you think they should return the excess to me? He said: If the assessment was in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you have no claim that they return the excess to you. I said: They assessed my date-palms; when I lifted my dates, they exceeded their assessment - do I pay them the excess? He said: No, if it was in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I said: What if I sell the fruit of my property before the assessor comes out - may they annul my sale and assess? He said: Yes, they assess it, if the assessment is in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); that is more entitled. Otherwise, you have sold for them and for you. And if he sells fruit for gold, charity is only on the fruit as it is, not on the gold."
"7036","From Ma'mar, who said: I said to Ayyub: I sold my fruit for two hundred dinars? He said: In it, for every ten dinars a dinar, if it has passed beyond reach. He said: But if it is reached in time, he takes from the fruit, and the buyer recovers from the seller a tenth of what he paid."
"7037","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: My property was assessed, then a calamity struck it and it perished before I secured it? He said: Nothing is upon you. He said: I said: So I placed it in the threshing floor, and it was stolen before I secured it? He said: Then no charity is upon you."
"7038","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim said to me: No one entertains an assessor as guest; if he does host him, the host bears no obligation of hospitality if he wishes, otherwise he milks, meaning he milks livestock for him."
"7039","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me that the assessors in that time would not host anyone as guests; they only ate from the property."
"7040","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent all his assessors - a man of the Ansar called Abdullah ibn al-Tayyihan, Abu al-Haytham - until when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, Abu Bakr sent him and he refused, saying: I used to assess for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then I would come and he would seek forgiveness for me; so who will seek forgiveness for me now? So Abu Bakr sent another man."
"7041","From Ma'mar, from Haram ibn Uthman, from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man of the Ansar from Banu Bayadah called Farwah ibn Amr, and he would assess the fruit of the people of Madinah. He said: And I have not heard of assessment except on date-palms and grapes."
"7042","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: How much is on what the sky waters, and what is watered by underground channels, of date-palms, grapes, or grain? He said: The tenth."
"7043","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: In it is the tenth."
"7044","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In what the sky waters, unirrigated land, rivers, and wellsprings: the tenth; and what is watered by drawing with buckets: half the tenth. Abd al-Razzaq said: Ba'l is athri land."
"7045","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, who said: What is watered by open flow or watered by the sky, in it is the tenth; and what is watered by camel-drawn bucket, half the tenth."
"7046","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, and from al-Zuhri, from Qatadah - Ma'mar said: And I read it in a letter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with each man to whom he wrote it: In what is watered by drawing and ropes, half the tenth. Ma'mar said: And I know of no disagreement in it. And in what was unirrigated, and what was by underground channels, and what was najl: the tenth. Ma'mar said: And I have not heard of disagreement in it."
"7047","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Madani, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: What rivers, sky, and springs water, the tenth; and what is watered by rope-drawn bucket, half the tenth."
"7048","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: How much is on what is watered by underground channels, what was unirrigated, and what was watered by open rivulets, of date-palms, grapes, or tilth? He said: The tenth. He said: I said: Then how much on what is watered by buckets and water-camels? He said: Half the tenth."
"7049","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: In what is watered by buckets and water-camels, half the tenth."
"7050","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata said to me: Everything whose watering needs no effort, in it is the tenth; and everything whose watering needs effort with the bucket, in it is half the tenth."
"7051","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he used to say: All charity on fruits and crops - of date-palms, grapes, or sown crops of wheat, barley, or hulless barley - what was unirrigated, or watered by river, or watered by spring, or rain-watered athri land, in it is the tenth, one in every ten; and what of it was watered by drawing, in it is half the tenth, one in every twenty. Ibn Jurayj said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen, to al-Harith ibn Abd Kulal and those with him of the people of Yemen, of Mu'afir and Hamadan: That upon the believers, of the charity on fruits, is the tenth on what spring and sky water, and on what is watered by camel-drawn bucket, half the tenth."
"7052","From Ma'mar, who said: Simak ibn al-Fadl gave me a letter from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Malik ibn Kaflanis and the Mus'abis, and I read it; in it was: In what sky and rivers water, the tenth; and in what is watered by water-wheel camel, half the tenth."
"7053","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Ten faraqs exceeding a hundred, watered by bucket, not a fraction - is there in them half a faraq? He did not permit me anything of it. And I say: Had there been anything in them, there would be in twenty dirhams exceeding two hundred dirhams half a dirham. He said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I consider that in every five faraqs exceeding a hundred there is charity, unlike the fraction of silver."
"7054","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Food from the provisions of these ships, or the Commander of the Faithful gave me of wheat or dates, and I kept it intending to eat it, and a year passes over it or over what remains of it? He said: No charity is upon you in it. By my life, we do that: we buy food and do not tax it. He said: But if you intend its sale, when you sell it, tax it."
"7055","From Ma'mar, who said: I asked Jabir al-Ju'fi about a man having food from his land intending its sale, its origin already taxed. He said: Al-Sha'bi said: There is no zakat in it until it is sold. He said: And al-Nakha'i said: There is zakat in it."
"7056","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim said to me: We say concerning tilth: when its zakat has been paid the first time and a year passes over it in your possession, do not tax it; the first suffices you. He said: And Ata said to me: The first suffices you. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I have not heard in it of other than the first."
"7057","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What about property that is on spring water most of the time, then needs the well and is watered by it, then returns to the spring - how is its charity? He said: The tenth. He said: Likewise property that is mostly so: if it is watered by spring more than it is watered by bucket, in it is the tenth; and if it is watered by bucket more than it is watered by open rivulet, in it is half the tenth. I said: And it is likewise by that status: property that is unirrigated or rain-watered most of the time, then once needs the well and is watered by bucket? He said: Yes."
"7058","From al-Thawri, concerning crops watered by camel-drawn bucket and by sky - upon which of them is its charity? He said: Upon that which gave it life, and upon that which prevailed over it is its charity."
"7059","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: My vegetables on a bucket, watered only by bucket always; I sold them for gold - how much in them? Is it half the tenth like crops? He said: No, it is gold like circulating gold: in every twenty dinars half a dinar, and in every forty dinars a dinar."
"7060","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: Muslim ibn Sukkarah informed me that he asked Ibn Umar: Do you know that Umar took tenths from the Muslims? He said: I do not know it; I do not know it."
"7061","Ma'mar informed us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs, and no charity in less than five dhawd, and no charity in less than five awaq."
"7062","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, who said: I heard, from more than one, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that he said: There is no charity in less than five awaq, and no charity in less than five wasqs of grain, and no charity in less than five wasqs of sweet produce."
"7063","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No charity in less than five awaq, nor in less than five wasqs, nor in less than five dhawd."
"7064","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah ibn Abi Husayn informed me, from his father Yahya ibn Umarah, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured with his palm with his five fingers: There is no charity in less than five awaq, and no charity in less than five wasqs, and no charity in less than five dhawd. Ibn Jurayj said, meaning dhawd of five camels. And he added, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith: And there is no charity on araya, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban."
"7065","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Yahya, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs, and no charity in less than five dhawd, and no charity in less than five awaq."
"7066","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from Yahya ibn Umarah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity on grain or fruit until it reaches five wasqs, and no charity in less than five awaq, and no charity in less than five dhawd. From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban, from Yahya ibn Umarah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"7067","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, and Qatadah, and Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and Ayyub, and Haram ibn Uthman from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and all of them mention it - they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs, nor in less than five awaq, nor in less than five dhawd."
"7068","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from a man of the family of Umar, from a man of the Ansar, from their fathers, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs, and no charity in less than five awaq - or he said: zakat - and no charity in less than five camels."
"7069","From Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs of dates, and no charity in less than five awaq of silver, and no charity in less than five dhawd of camels."
"7070","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: The wasq is sixty sa's, and five wasqs are three hundred sa's."
"7071","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, who said: The wasq is sixty sa's. Sufyan said: By the first sa'."
"7072","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no charity in less than five wasqs. I said to him: How much is the sa'? He said: Four mudds by the mudd of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: How much is the mudd? He said: Some of them said: A ritl and a half, and some of them said: Two ritls. From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about the wasqs, and he verified them for me."
"7073","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata, concerning And give its due on the day of its harvest - is it for everything, and do not be extravagant in what you give of the due on the day of its harvest, or in everything? He said: Yes, in everything He forbids extravagance, and in everything there is innocence; as for His saying, And give its due on the day of its harvest, it is of date-palms, grapes, and all grain. I said: What about fruits? He said: In them also they give. Then he said: Of everything harvested they give its due on the day of its harvest - of date-palms, grapes, grain, fruit, vegetables, reeds, or of everything of that. He said: That is repeatedly. I said: Is that obligatory on people? He said: Yes. Then he recited: And give its due on the day of its harvest. Then I said: Is anything described and fixed? He said: No. I said: If I give charity of what I pay, little charity or much, does it suffice me? He said: Yes, it suffices you. I said: If no poor are present with me, shall I store for them? He said: Yes, or you send to your neighbors. He said: Does it suffice me when I give my neighbor? He said: Yes, if he is needy. He said: I said: I had assorted grain - millet, hulless barley, dates, barley, and assorted grain - and I reckoned all that together as produce; shall I feed from every kind of grain, or does it suffice me to feed from each one? He said: Rather feed from every kind of grain. He said: That is repeatedly. I said to him: What is dukhn? He said: A grain in Ta'if; and sult is like barley without husk, and it is saqah."
"7074","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning Allah's saying, And give its due on the day of its harvest, he said: At sowing he gives the pinch, and at harvest he gives the handful, and he leaves them to follow the traces of reaping. I said: What is the handful? He said: A fistful of ears. We said: What is the pinch? He said: When you sow, you give them of the scattered seed with your fingertips, and he gestured with them."
"7075","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, who said: At the harvest of dates, the bunch would be cut and people would eat from it."
"7076","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, concerning His saying, And give its due on the day of its harvest, they said: Zakat."
"7077","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed us, from Amr ibn Sulaym, and from others, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he said, And give its due on the day of its harvest, he said: The obligatory charity. Sa'id said: And His saying, And do not be extravagant, he said: Do not withhold charity, lest you disobey. Ibn Jurayj said: And others said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal reaped his date-palms and kept giving charity from his dates until nothing remained of them, so there came down: And do not be extravagant."
"7078","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What if a man buys from a man the fruit of his garden, whatever it was, and harvests - which of them pays the due of what he harvested, the seller or the buyer? He said: The buyer; he said it more than once. I reviewed with him concerning charity, on which of them is it? He said: On the buyer, if they had not stipulated it; he said it more than once. I said to him: From where is this taken? He said: It is a garden in which there is charity; he bought it and sees charity due on it, unless he stipulated that it is not upon him. And he had said to me after that: If he leaves the whole garden, charity is upon its owner."
"7079","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim said to me: He used to forbid that the gate of the garden be closed on the day the date-palms are gathered and the grapes picked, because of the poor, who eat what falls of dates and grapes; yet he does not leave them free with all that falls, but they leave until the wandering man takes the grain; likewise they leave what falls of ears after they are rewarded from it with kindness. He said: And that has sufficed you when you lift it from the threshing floor. Abd al-Razzaq said: Ha'im is the exhausted; yujazawna means they are given."
"7080","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No date-palms are harvested by night, and no milk is mixed with water for sale."
"7081","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade lifting the threshing floor by night, and reaping by night."
"7082","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman spends from her husband's earnings without his order, he has half its reward."
"7083","From Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah, that he was asked about a woman giving charity from her husband's wealth, and he said: No, except from her sustenance; and the reward is between her and her husband. And it is not lawful for her to give charity from her husband's wealth except with his permission. And Ibn Jurayj informed me from Ata the like of it."
"7084","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman spends from her husband's food without spoiling, she has her reward, and her husband has the like, and neither diminishes the other at all; and the storekeeper has the like: hers for what she spent, and his for what he earned."
"7085","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from a woman: that she was with A'ishah, and a woman asked her: May a woman give charity from her husband's house? She said: Yes, so long as she does not guard her wealth with his wealth."
"7086","From Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage: Allah has given every holder of right his right, so there is no bequest for an heir; the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone; and their reckoning is upon Allah. Whoever claims other than his father and allies other than his patrons, upon him is the successive curse of Allah until the Day of Resurrection. And a woman must not spend anything from her house except with her husband's permission. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, not even food? He said: That is the best of our wealth. He said: Then he said: A loaned item is to be returned, a granted milch animal is to be given back, a debt is to be paid, and the guarantor is liable."
"7087","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, who said: A woman came to him and said: Is it lawful for me to take from my husband's dirhams? He said: Is it lawful for him to take from your jewelry? She said: No. He said: Then he has greater right over you."
"7088","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'amah, that he heard Ibn Abbas say: It is not lawful for a woman to give charity from her husband's house except with his permission."
"7089","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: People are not made to swear over their charities; whoever pays anything, it is accepted from him."
"7090","From al-Thawri, who said: No one is made to swear by the mushaf; whoever pays anything, it is accepted from him. And they are trusted over their zakat as they are trusted over their prayer. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Raja ibn Rawh wrote to al-Thawri: Are people made to swear over their zakat by the mushaf? So he wrote to him with this, while al-Thawri was in Makkah."
"7091","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: No one is made to swear by the mushaf; and Ma'mar used to dislike that anyone be made to swear by the mushaf."
"7092","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman used to say: The owner of the wealth pays what he mentions of wealth, and his wealth is not summoned, nor is he made to swear."
"7093","He said: I heard al-Thawri say: Deceive them, swear to them, lie to them, scheme against them, and give them nothing when they do not place it in its proper places. Abd al-Razzaq said: And this hadith is not sound."
"7094","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Jubayr ibn Muhammad, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not keep wealth with him at noon nor overnight. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: When you give, enrich."
"7095","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Umar ibn al-Khattab gathered people of the Muslims and said: I wish to place this fay in its place, so let every man of you come to me in the morning with his opinion. When morning came he said: I have found a verse of Allah's Book - or he said: verses - Allah has left no Muslim having any share in this wealth without naming him. Allah said: And know that whatever you gain, a fifth of it is for Allah and for the Messenger, until He reached: And whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain. Then he recited: For the poor Emigrants who were expelled from their homes, up to: those are the truthful - this is for the Emigrants. Then he recited: And those who settled the home and faith before them, up to: And whoever is saved from the stinginess of his soul, those are the successful - then he said: this is for the Ansar. Then he recited: And those who came after them, saying: Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith, up to: Kind, Merciful. Then he said: There is no Muslim on earth without a right in this wealth, given or withheld."
"7096","From al-Nu'man ibn Abi Shaybah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: There is no charity on endowed charity, meaning zakat. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: Why? He said: Because he made it for the poor; and when they take something from it, is the poor man not given? We said: Yes. He said: Then there is no charity in it."
"7097","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, who said: My father had a levy on his land which he paid every year, whether it produced anything or not; so he spoke to the governor, or it was spoken for him, and he said: We shall drop it for you and place it on another. But he refused and kept paying it. Abd al-Razzaq said: And one of the people of knowledge narrated to me, he said: Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman used to say: When they drop it from me, they may place it on whom they wish."
"7098","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: The child is ordered to pray when he knows his right from his left, and to fast when he can bear it. From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Ibn Sirin the like of it. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah the like of it."
"7099","From Ibn Jurayj, and Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, who said: My father used to order boys to pray when they understood it, and to fast when they could bear it."
"7100","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, who said: The boy is ordered to pray before fasting, for prayer is easier."
"7101","From al-Thawri, from Abu Raja, from Makhul, who said: He is beaten for it at ten years, and ordered with it at seven years."
"7102","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: The child used to be ordered to pray when his front teeth fell out."
"7103","From al-Thawri, from Marzuq, who said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyab: When is prayer prescribed for a girl? He said: When she menstruates. He said: I said: And the boy? He said: When he has a wet dream."
"7104","From Ibn al-Mubarak, who said: Husayn ibn Abdullah narrated to me, who said: Umm Yasin, servant of Ibn Abbas, narrated to me that Ibn Abbas used to say: Wake the child to pray, even if with a single prostration."
"7105","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: Abdullah said: Guard your children in prayer."
"7106","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Lubayba, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When a boy fasts three consecutive days, fasting the month of Ramadan has become obligatory upon him."
"7107","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If you do not see the crescent of Ramadan, complete Shaban as thirty days, and if you do not see the crescent of Shawwal, complete Ramadan as thirty days."
"7108","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that he heard Muhammad ibn Hunayn say: Ibn Abbas used to disapprove of entering into the fasting of Ramadan in advance when they had not seen the crescent of the month of Ramadan, and he would say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When you do not see the crescent, complete thirty days."
"7109","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Count the crescent of Shaban for sighting the month of Ramadan; when you see it, fast, then when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured from you, complete the number."
"7110","From Mamar, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said concerning the crescent of Ramadan: When you see it, fast, then when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured from you, complete thirty; your fast is the day you fast, and your breaking fast is the day you break fast. And Ibn Jurayj added in this hadith: And your sacrifice is the day you sacrifice."
"7111","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama and Ibn al-Musayyib, or one of them, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured from you, fast thirty."
"7112","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Allah has made the crescents time-markers for people, so fast at its sighting and break fast at its sighting; if it is obscured from you, count for it thirty days."
"7113","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said concerning the crescent of the month of Ramadan: When you see it, fast, then when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured from you, estimate for it thirty days."
"7114","From al-Thawri, from Humayd, who said: Al-Walid ibn Utba al-Laythi narrated to us, saying: We fasted with Ali twenty-eight days, then on the day of breaking fast he ordered us to make up one day."
"7115","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who said: I have not fasted twenty-nine more often than I have fasted thirty."
"7116","From Mamar, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from al-Hakam, or another, from Masruq, that he and a man with him entered upon Aisha on the day of Arafa, and Aisha said: O slave girl, stir sawiq for them and sweeten it; were I not fasting I would taste it. They said: Do you fast, O Mother of the Believers, while you do not know, perhaps it is the day of sacrifice? She said: Sacrifice is only when the Imam and the generality of people sacrifice, and breaking fast is when the Imam and the generality of people break fast."
"7117","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me, saying: I was with Ibn Abbas a day or two before Ramadan, and his lunch was brought near, and he said: Break fast, O you fasting people; do not join anything to Ramadan, and separate. He said: And Ibn Abd al-Qari was fasting, and I thought he broke fast."
"7118","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, saying: Ibn Abbas used to command a separation between them."
"7119","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: Do not join anything to Ramadan, and separate."
"7120","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Does the day of breaking fast suffice you to separate with? He said: No. He said: Days before it or after it."
"7121","He said: Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade anticipating the month of Ramadan by fasting a day or two, except a man who used to fast a fast and that falls upon his fast."
"7122","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Separate between Shaban and Ramadan by breaking fast a day or two or about that."
"7123","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: They woke one morning doubting about fasting, and that was in Ramadan, so I went early to Anas ibn Malik and found he had gone out early for a need, so I asked his family, saying: Did he wake fasting or breaking fast? They said: He drank kharida then went out. He said: Then I entered upon Muslim ibn Yasar and he called for lunch. He said: I did not enter upon any man of our companions that day except I saw him breaking fast, except one man; I wished he had not done it. He said: And I think he followed calculation."
"7124","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from a man, who said: We were with Ammar ibn Yasir on the day doubted about in Ramadan, and a roasted sheep was brought, and a man from the people withdrew. He said: Come near. He said: I am fasting, and it is only a fast I used to fast. He said: As for you, do you believe in Allah and the Last Day? Then eat."
"7125","From al-Thawri, from Simak, from Ikrima, who said: I saw him order a man after noon to break fast, saying: Whoever fasts this day has disobeyed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"7126","From al-Thawri, from Abu Abbad, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade fasting six days: a day before Ramadan, Adha, Fitr, and the three days of Tashriq."
"7127","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muzahim informed me, saying: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz addressed during his caliphate, saying: Watch for the crescent of Ramadan; if you see it, fast, and if you do not see it, complete thirty days. He said: And people woke, some fasting and some breaking fast, and they had not seen the crescent, then news came to them that the crescent had been seen. He said: People spoke to Umar, and he sent the guards among the army: Whoever woke fasting, let him complete his fast, for he has been guided to it; whoever woke breaking fast and has not tasted anything, let him complete the rest of his day; whoever ate something, let him complete what remains of his day, and make up a day in its place afterwards. For I have licked a lick of honey today, so I am fasting the rest of my day, then I will replace it afterwards."
"7128","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If a man wakes breaking fast and has not tasted anything, then learns of its sighting at the beginning or end of the day, let him fast what remains, and not replace it."
"7129","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Umar, that when it was cloudy he would wake fasting, and when there was no cloud he would wake breaking fast. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the like of it."
"7130","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yaqub, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When it is the middle of Shaban, break fast."
"7131","Dawud ibn Qays informed us, saying: I asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about fasting the day doubted about in Ramadan. He said: If it is cloudy, intending it as part of Ramadan, let him not fast it."
"7132","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A traveler enters a town having woken breaking fast, but he has not tasted anything? He said: He completes it."
"7133","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Amr ibn Dinar: Is it not said to one who has intercourse with his wife in Ramadan: Let him complete that day then make it up, and likewise one who has intercourse with his wife during Hajj? He said: Yes."
"7134","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, who said: Habib ibn al-Shahid informed me, saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Sirin say: That I break fast a day of Ramadan without intending it is dearer to me than fasting the day doubted about in Shaban. Jafar said: And Asma ibn Ubayd informed me, saying: We came to Muhammad ibn Sirin on the day doubted about, and said: What should we do? He said to his slave: Go see whether the Amir fasted or not. He said: And the Amir that day was Adi ibn Artat. He returned to him and said: I found him breaking fast. He said: Then Muhammad called for his lunch and ate, and we ate with him."
"7135","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to him: A man breaking fast on the day doubted about, then news comes? He said: He eats and drinks."
"7136","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, who said: Umar wrote to us while we were at Khanqin: If you see the crescent in daytime, do not break fast until two men testify that they saw it yesterday."
"7137","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Shibak, from Ibrahim, who said: Umar wrote to Utba ibn Farqad: If you see the crescent in daytime before the sun declines, completing thirty, then break fast; if you see it after the sun has declined, do not break fast until evening."
"7138","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz disliked for people who saw the crescent at the end of the day to eat anything."
"7139","Al-Hasan ibn Umara said: Al-Hakam informed me, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali, who said: If you see the crescent at the beginning of the day, break fast; if you see it at the end of the day, do not break fast, for the sun turns away from it."
"7140","From al-Thawri, from Rukayn ibn al-Rabi, from his father Rabi ibn Umayla, who said: We were with Salman ibn Rabia al-Bahili at Balangar. He said: I saw the crescent in the forenoon, completing thirty, so I came to Salman ibn Rabia and told him, and he came with me and I showed it to him from shade under a tree, and he ordered the people and they broke fast."
"7141","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from one of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: People woke fasting in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then two bedouins came and testified by Allah besides whom there is no god, saying: Thus we indeed saw it yesterday. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered the people, and they broke fast."
"7142","We said to Mamar: What do you think: if two men testify that they saw it yesterday, testifying at the end of the day, having come from a journey, do people break fast that evening? He said: Yes, and they go out the next morning."
"7143","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ribi, from one of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it, with addition: And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not precede the crescent of this month until you see the crescent, or complete the number before it; then fast, and do not break fast until you see the crescent, or complete the number after it."
"7144","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba: That two men saw the crescent while on a journey, and hastened until they reached Medina in the forenoon and told Umar ibn al-Khattab of that. Umar said to one of them: Are you fasting? He said: Yes. He said: Why? He said: Because I disliked that people be fasting while I am breaking fast, so I disliked differing from them. He said to the other: And you? He said: I woke breaking fast. He said: Why? He said: Because I saw the crescent and disliked fasting. He said to the one who broke fast: Were it not for this, meaning the one who fasted, we would have rejected your testimony and hurt your head. Then he ordered the people and they broke fast and went out."
"7145","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, who said: Abu Bishr Jafar ibn Abi Wahshiyya narrated to me, that Abu Umayr ibn Anas narrated to him, saying: Uncles of mine from the Ansar, from the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, informed me, saying: The crescent of Shawwal was obscured from us, so we woke fasting, then riders came at the end of the day to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and testified that they had seen the crescent yesterday. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered the people to break fast for the rest of their day, and to go out for their Eid the next morning."
"7146","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Musa informed me, from Nafi, who said: The crescent of Shawwal was seen in daytime, but Abdullah did not break fast until evening, and went out to the prayer place the next morning."
"7147","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: I woke fasting, then news of its sighting came at the end of the day? He said: Break fast. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: They break fast whenever the news comes to them."
"7148","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrima, that a bedouin came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have seen the crescent. He said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered Bilal, who announced among the people to fast."
"7149","From al-Thawri, who said: I heard him, or one who heard him narrate, from Abd al-Ala, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that Umar ibn al-Khattab accepted the testimony of one man concerning sighting the crescent for breaking fast or sacrifice."
"7150","From a man of the people of Medina, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to accept one man for fasting upon sighting the crescent, but would not accept for breaking fast except two men."
"7151","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: None is valid for fasting, breaking fast, and sacrifice except two men."
"7152","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: None is valid concerning sighting the crescent except two men."
"7153","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Amr ibn Dinar narrate: That Uthman refused to accept Hashim ibn Utba al-Awar alone concerning sighting the crescent of Ramadan."
"7154","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: What do you think: if a man sees the crescent of Ramadan a night before the people, does he fast before them or break fast before them? He said: No, unless the people see it; I fear it was confused to him, until they are two. He said: I said: No, except he saw it and watched it for a while. He said: Even so, until they are two."
"7155","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I was told of Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, that a man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: I saw the crescent of the month of Ramadan. He said: Did another see it with you? He said: No. He said: What did you do? He said: I fasted with the fasting of the people. Umar said: O what understanding you have."
"7156","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: While a man was traveling in a desert land, a crescent appeared, and he kept looking at it, when he heard a speaker saying, whom he did not see: O Allah, bring it upon us with security and faith, safety and Islam, guidance and forgiveness, success for what You love, and protection from what angers You; my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He kept repeating it until the man memorized it."
"7157","Mamar informed us, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he saw the crescent, would say: I believe in the One who created you, fashioned you, and proportioned you."
"7158","Mamar informed us, saying: A man informed me, that a man told him himself, saying: While I was traveling I saw the crescent, and I heard a speaker saying, whom I did not see: O Allah, bring it upon us with safety and Islam, security and faith, goodness and piety, and success for what You love and approve. He kept repeating it until I memorized it."
"7159","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he saw the crescent, would magnify Allah three times, then say: A crescent of good and guidance, three times, then say: I believe in the One who created you, three times, then say: Praise be to Allah who took away such-and-such month and brought such-and-such month."
"7160","From al-Thawri, who said: It reached me, from Ibrahim, that he used to say concerning a traveler arriving breaking fast, or a menstruating woman becoming pure at the end of her day: They should not eat until evening."
"7161","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muzahim, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, who said: He should not eat until evening."
"7162","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, and Mamar, from Qatada, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: They were asked about a menstruating woman becoming pure before sunset. They said: She eats and drinks."
"7163","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrima, who said: She should neither eat nor drink."
"7164","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a woman who wakes fasting while menstruating: He said: If she becomes pure at the beginning of the day, let her complete her day; otherwise not."
"7165","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a Christian or Jew who accepts Islam during part of Ramadan: He said: He fasts what remains of the month."
"7166","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, who said: If a Christian accepts Islam during part of Ramadan, he fasts what has passed of it; if he accepts Islam at the end of the day, he fasts that day."
"7167","From a man, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrima, concerning a Christian who accepts Islam with days remaining of Ramadan: He said: He fasts what he reaches, and makes up what passed him; if he accepts Islam on the last day of Ramadan, he is like a traveler joining the prayer of residents."
"7168","From Mamar, from al-Hasan, who says: If he accepts Islam during part of the month of Ramadan, he fasts all of it. And the saying of Qatada is dearer to me."
"7169","From al-Thawri, concerning a Christian who accepts Islam at the end of the day: He said: Whoever takes the saying of Ata says: He prays Zuhr and Asr; whoever takes the saying of al-Hasan says: He prays Asr and does not pray Zuhr. And he said: When he accepts Islam in the month of Ramadan, he does not fast his day in which he accepted Islam, but he is ordered not to eat until evening."
"7170","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, who said: He fasts the day in which he accepted Islam."
"7171","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who said: If two men look at the dawn and one of them doubts, let them both eat until it is clear to them."
"7172","Wahb ibn Nafi informed us, from Ikrima, freedman of Ibn Abbas, who said: Give me drink, O boy. He said: Morning has come. I said: No. Ibn Abbas said: A doubt, by Allah: Give me drink. So he drank."
"7173","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, who said: Ibn Abbas said: Allah has made drink lawful for you as long as you doubt, until you do not doubt."
"7174","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Muslim ibn Subayh, who said: A man said to Ibn Abbas: What do you think: if I doubt about the dawn while intending to fast? He said: Eat as long as you doubt, until you do not doubt."
"7175","From Ibn Uyayna, from Israil Abu Musa, from al-Hasan, who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, the caller to prayer called while a vessel was in my hand, and I intend fasting. He said: Drink."
"7176","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Hayyan ibn Umayr, who said: Ibn Abbas was asked about a man hearing the adhan while night is upon him. He said: Let him eat. It was said: But he heard another caller. He said: One of them testified for the other."
"7177","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: Do you dislike that I drink while in the house, not knowing, perhaps I have woken? He said: There is no harm in that; it is doubt."
"7178","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, who said: Whoever eats forgetfully or drinks forgetfully, there is no harm upon him; Allah fed him and gave him drink. And Qatada used to say it."
"7179","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If a man tastes forgetfully, let him complete his fast and not make it up; Allah fed him and gave him drink."
"7180","And from Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: He completes his fast and does not make it up; Allah fed him and gave him drink."
"7181","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: If a man has intercourse with his wife while fasting forgetfully in Ramadan, there is nothing upon him for it."
"7182","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked Ata about a man who has intercourse with his wife forgetfully in Ramadan. He said: All this is not forgotten; upon him is making up; Allah has made no excuse for him."
"7183","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hasan, who said: He is like one who eats and drinks forgetfully."
"7184","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that a man came to Abu Hurayra and said: I woke fasting, then forgot, and ate and drank. He said: No harm; Allah fed you and gave you drink. He said: Then I entered upon another man and forgot, and ate and drank. He said: No harm; Allah fed you and gave you drink. He said: Then I entered upon another man and forgot and ate. Abu Hurayra said: You are a man unaccustomed to fasting."
"7185","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A man rinses his nose and water enters his throat. He said: There is no harm in that. And Mamar said it, from Qatada."
"7186","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim, or another, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who rinses his mouth while fasting and water enters his throat: He said: If it was for the obligatory prayer, there is no making up upon him; if it was voluntary, upon him is making up. Sufyan said: And making up is dearer to me in every case."
"7187","A man informed us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, concerning a man who rinses his mouth while fasting and it enters his stomach: He said: If it was for the obligatory prayer, there is nothing upon him; if it was voluntary, upon him is making up. From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"7188","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said of the month of Ramadan: This month has come, and it is a blessed month; Allah has obligated its fasting; in it the gates of Hell are closed, the gates of Paradise are opened, the devils are chained; in it is a night better than a thousand months; whoever is deprived of it is indeed deprived."
"7189","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Abi Anas, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When the month of Ramadan enters, the gates of mercy are opened, the gates of Hell are closed, and the devils are chained."
"7190","From Mamar, from Aban, from Said ibn Jubayr, who said: I think, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When the first night of the month of Ramadan enters, the gates of Paradise are opened and no gate of them is closed the whole month, the gates of Fire are closed and no gate of them is opened the whole month, the rebellious jinn are chained; then Allah has freed ones whom He frees from the Fire, at the time of every breaking fast, slaves and slave women."
"7191","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Arfaja, who said: We were mentioning the month of Ramadan, when Utba ibn Farqad said: What are you mentioning? He said: We were mentioning the month of Ramadan. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: In it the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of Fire are closed, the devils are chained, and a caller calls every night: O seeker of good, come; O seeker of evil, desist."
"7192","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: I broke fast on a cloudy day in Ramadan thinking it the first of the night, then the sun appeared. He said: Make up that day. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, the like of it."
"7193","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: If a man breaks fast in Ramadan then the sun appears, upon him is to make it up; if he eats in the morning thinking it night, he does not make it up. From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, the like of it. And Ibn Jurayj said it, from Ata."
"7194","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Said ibn Jubayr, who said: He completes it, and makes up a day in its place; if he eats thinking night is upon him, when it is morning, upon him is making up."
"7195","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from his father, who said: People broke fast in Ramadan on a cloudy day, then a looker looked and the sun was there. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The matter is easy, and we have striven; we make up a day."
"7196","From Jabala ibn Suhaym, from Ali ibn Hanzala, from his father, who said: We were with Umar ibn al-Khattab in Ramadan, and a large bowl was brought. The caller to prayer said: O people, the sun has risen. Umar said: May Allah protect us, or free us, from your evil; we did not send you as a watcher of the sun, but we sent you as a caller to prayer. O people, whoever broke fast, making up a day is easy; whoever has not broken fast, let him complete his fast."
"7197","From al-Thawri, who said: Ziyad ibn Ilaqa narrated to me, from Bishr ibn Qays, who said: We were with Umar ibn al-Khattab in Ramadan while the sky was cloudy, and sawiq was brought and the sun rose. He said: Whoever broke fast, let him make up a day in its place. Abd al-Razzaq said: And a companion of ours informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, from Bishr, the like of it, except he said: Umar said: Complete this day of yours, then make up a day."
"7198","Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: People broke fast in the time of Umar. He said: I saw large cups brought out from Hafsa's house, and they drank in Ramadan, then the sun rose from clouds. It was as if that weighed upon people, and they said: Do we make up this day? Umar said: And why? By Allah, we did not incline to sin. And in Umar's other hadith: He ordered making it up."
"7199","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is overtaken by morning while junub has no fast. He said: So I and my father set out and entered upon Aisha and Umm Salama and asked them about that, and they told us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to wake junub without a wet dream, then fast. He said: Then we entered upon Marwan and told him their saying and Abu Hurayra's saying. He said: I adjure you to go to Abu Hurayra and tell him their saying. He said: So Abu Hurayra met us at the mosque door, and my father said to him: The Amir has adjured us in a matter to mention to you. He said: What is it? He said: So my father told him. He said: Abu Hurayra's face changed color, then said: Thus al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, and he knows better. Al-Zuhri said: So he transferred the hadith to another."
"7200","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Umm Salama and Aisha, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would be overtaken by dawn while junub from his wives, then bathe and fast."
"7201","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his father, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say in his stories: Whoever is overtaken by dawn while junub has no fast. Then he mentioned the like of Mamar's hadith, from al-Zuhri."
"7202","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that Yahya ibn Jada informed him, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Qari, that he heard Abu Hurayra say: By the Lord of this House, whoever is overtaken by morning while junub, let him break fast. But Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said."
"7203","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Does a man spend the night junub in Ramadan deliberately until morning, then fast? He said: As for Abu Hurayra, he used to forbid that; as for Aisha, she used to say: There is no harm in that. When they differed to Ata, he said: He completes that day of his, and replaces a day."
"7204","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud said: I do not care if I go to my wife then wake junub then fast; I went to what is lawful."
"7205","From al-Thawri, from Jami ibn Abi Rashid, who said: Abd Allah ibn Mirdas narrated to us, saying: A man of the tribe came to me and said: I passed by my wife in moonlight and she pleased me, so I had intercourse with her in Ramadan, then slept until morning. I said: Go to Abd Allah ibn Masud, or Abu Hakim al-Muzani. So he went to Abd Allah and asked him. He said: You were junub, prayer was not lawful for you, so you bathed and prayer became lawful for you, and fasting became lawful for you, so fast."
"7206","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: A man came to Abu al-Darda and said: I went to my wife, then my eyes overcame me until morning, while I intend fasting. Abu al-Darda said: You went to your wife while she was lawful to you, then you were overcome, then Allah returned your soul; so you prayed when you understood, and fasted when you understood."
"7207","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, who said: If the caller to prayer called while Abd Allah was between his wife's legs, intending to fast, he would complete his fast."
"7208","From Ibn Uyayna, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, who said: Whoever is overtaken by morning junub deliberately for that, let him replace the fast; whoever comes to that unintentionally, let him not replace it."
"7209","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to forbid kissing by one fasting. It was said to him: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to kiss while fasting. He said: And who has the guarding and protection that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has."
"7210","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Abbas: That the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to kiss while fasting. Then Ibn Abbas said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would take from the heads while fasting, meaning kissing."
"7211","From Mamar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aisha, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to kiss some of his wives while fasting. From Mamar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, the like of it."
"7212","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Talha ibn Abd Allah ibn Uthman, who said: I heard Aisha say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took me to kiss me, and I said: I am fasting. He said: And I am fasting. Then he kissed me."
"7213","From Malik ibn Anas, from Abu al-Nadr, from Aisha bint Talha, that she told him: She was with Aisha, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when her husband, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman, entered upon her, fasting in Ramadan. Aisha said to him: What prevents you from drawing near your wife, fondling her and kissing her? He said: Do I kiss her while fasting? She said: Yes."
"7214","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Zayd ibn Aslam informed me, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a man of the Ansar, that he told him: That he kissed his wife in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while fasting, so he ordered his wife and she asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about that. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, does that. His wife told him, and he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is given concession in things, so return to him and say that to him. She returned to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I am the most God-fearing of you, and most knowledgeable of you of Allah's limits."
"7215","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas asked about kissing for one fasting. He said: There is no harm in it if he stops at it. It was said to him: Does he grasp her leg? He said again: Spare the fasting one; he should not grasp her leg. From Umar ibn Habib, that he heard Ata say: I heard Ibn Abbas say the like of Ibn Jurayj's hadith."
"7216","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: There is no harm in it if there is nothing else with it, meaning kissing."
"7217","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, who said: He was asked about kissing for one fasting. He said: It is a guide to other than it, and keeping away is wiser."
"7218","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, who said: One who heard the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, narrated to me: They used to forbid one another kissing while fasting, saying: Perhaps you will be called to greater than it."
"7219","Mamar informed us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: An old man came to Ibn Abbas asking him about kissing while fasting, and he gave him concession; then a youth came to him and he forbade him. From Mamar, and Qatada used to narrate it, from Ibn Abbas, from a man of the people of Medina, from Yunus ibn Sayf, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar, the like of Ibn Abbas's saying."
"7220","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: It was said to Abu Hurayra: Do you kiss while fasting? He said: Yes, and I press mouth to mouth, meaning he opens his mouth to hers. He said: It was said to Sad ibn Malik: Do you kiss while fasting? He said: Yes, and I seize her belongings."
"7221","From Ibn Jurayj, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Said al-Maqburi, that a man asked Abu Hurayra, saying: A man kissed his wife while fasting, has he broken fast? He said: No. He said: Other than it? He said: So Abu Hurayra turned away."
"7222","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to forbid kissing for one fasting. From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"7223","From al-Thawri, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Zad han, who said: Ibn Umar was asked: Does a man kiss while fasting? He said: Would he not kiss a coal."
"7224","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from al-Hazh az, from Ibn Masud, concerning a man who kisses while fasting: He said: He makes up a day in its place. Sufyan said: And this is not acted upon."
"7225","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh: That a man kissed his wife while fasting, and he said: Fear Allah, and do not repeat."
"7226","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Umar ibn Said, who said: Ali said concerning kissing for one fasting: I do not see harm in abstaining from it."
"7227","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar: That Atika bint Zayd kissed Umar ibn al-Khattab while he was fasting, and he did not forbid her. He said: And I think he said: While he intended to go out to prayer."
"7228","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ruzayq, and Khusayf, that they asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a man kissing his wife while fasting. He said: If you kiss, it does not break your fast, but it diminishes your fast."
"7229","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to kiss while fasting."
"7230","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Touching by one fasting and stripping is forbidden."
"7231","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that what one who touches or strips diminishes from his fast; you may take her hand and her nearer body, and leave her farther part."
"7232","From Mamar, from Alqama ibn Abi Alqama, who said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a man who embraces while fasting? He said: He repents ten times."
"7233","From Mamar, who said: One who heard Ikrima informed us, saying concerning embracing for one fasting: There is no harm in it; it is only like a morsel he smelled. He said: Allah has made lawful that he take her hand and her nearer body, and not take her farther part."
"7234","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: He embraces her lying open in daytime? He said: His fast is not nullified, but let him replace a day in place of that day, and not break fast. I said: He embraced her lying open until morning? He said: There is no harm in that, whether warming himself or not, nothing exited from him. Then he said afterwards: If it was with dawn, then no."
"7235","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: He embraced her in daytime, her upper part. He said: Let him not do. I said: He embraces her in daytime with a garment between them? He said: As for anything of that done deliberately, no."
"7236","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: He used to forbid embracing for one fasting."
"7237","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Masruq, who said: I asked Aisha: What is lawful for a man of his wife while fasting? She said: Everything except intercourse."
"7238","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, who said: I saw al-Hasan meet Abu Rafi, saying: I was between them. He said: Al-Hasan said to him: One fasting kisses and embraces. Abu Rafi said: He neither kisses nor embraces."
"7239","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: We went out as pilgrims, and mentioned: One fasting kisses and embraces. A man of Nakha who had fasted two years and stood them, leaning on a staff, said: I have intended to take this bow of mine and strike you with it. They came to Aisha, and said to Alqama: O Abu Shibl. He said: I am not one to speak intimately with her today. She heard him and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to kiss and embrace while fasting; he was the most master of you of his desire."
"7240","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zakariyya, from al-Shabi, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, that Ibn Masud used to embrace his wife at midday while fasting."
"7241","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayra say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Fasting is a shield; when one of you is fasting a day, let him not act ignorantly nor speak foully; if a man fights him, let him say: I am fasting."
"7242","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrima, who said: Sad ibn Malik used to rub her front with his hand while fasting."
"7243","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: He seized her front lying open. He said: Let him not do; if he does, let him not replace a day in place of that day."
"7244","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: Does he feel, and touch what is under the garment? He said: No. I said: What is above the garment? He said: I do not like that."
"7245","Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: I said to Ata: What do you think: if he uncovered and examined and sat between her legs, then withdrew and the gushing water did not come from him? He said: His fast is not nullified, but let him replace a day in place of that day, and not break it."
"7246","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: If he intended to fulfill his need short of her front, then withdrew, and the gushing water did not come from him? He said: His fast is not nullified, but let him make up a day in place of that day, and not break it."
"7247","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: I do not know but he said: If the gushing comes with his fondling, upon him is what is upon one who has intercourse."
"7248","From Mamar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who kisses in daytime in Ramadan, or embraces, or handles and emits madhi: He said: There is nothing upon him, but evil is what he did; if the gushing water exits from him, he is like one who has intercourse. He said: And Qatada said: If the gushing exits from him, nothing is upon him except to fast a day."
"7249","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If a man fondles his wife while fasting until the gushing comes from him, upon him is expiation. He said: I said to Ata: What do you think: what stirs a fasting man's organ in Ramadan, with madhi exiting with its stirring? He said: There is nothing upon him in that, unless there was embracing or something approaching that."
"7250","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Talha ibn Musarrif, from Khaythama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, who said: Whoever contemplates a woman's form while fasting, his fast is nullified."
"7251","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Malik ibn Mighwal and others, he said: Isa ibn Maryam said: The glance sows desire in the heart, and it suffices its owner as a trial."
"7252","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: I heard Qays ibn Abi Hazim saying: You have no right to keep staring at a woman while her clothes are on her."
"7253","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does not give up lying and foul speech, Allah has no need for him to give up his food and drink - meaning the fasting person."
"7254","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you that a person when invited to food is commanded to say: I am fasting? He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: When you are fasting, do not act foolishly nor trade insults, and if someone acts foolishly against you, say: I am fasting."
"7255","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined. He said: What is the matter? He said: I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan. He said: Can you find a slave to free? He said: No. He said: Can you fast two consecutive months? He said: No. He said: Then feed sixty poor persons. He said: I cannot find, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then a basket containing dates was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and an araq is a large basket. He said: Go and give this in charity. He said: Upon one poorer than me? By the One who sent you with the truth, there is no household between its two lava plains more needy than me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed, then said: Take it to your family. Al-Zuhri said: This was only a concession for that man specifically; if a man did that today, expiation would be unavoidable."
"7256","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the wretched other is ruined. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the matter? He said: I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Can you free a slave? He said: No. He said: Then offer a camel as sacrifice. He said: I cannot find it. He said: Then a basket containing fifteen sa' was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Give this in charity. He complained of need to him, so he said: Upon you and your family - or he said: twenty sa'."
"7257","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata al-Khurasani informed me, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: A bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) beating his chest and pulling his hair, saying: The far wretch is ruined. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What is your case? He said: I had intercourse in the month of Ramadan. He said: Can you free a slave? He said: No. He said: Then offer a sacrifice. He said: You mean a camel? He said: It is nothing but that. He said: I cannot find it. He said: Sit down. He said: So he sat, then a man came with a basket containing twenty sa' of dates, or fifteen sa', and he said to the bedouin: Give this in charity. He complained of need to him, so he said: Upon you and your family."
"7258","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Ma'mar from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Mashar al-Madani, from Muhammad ibn Kab: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to fast a day in its place when he commanded him with the expiation."
"7259","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi ibn Jubayr: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Give charity, and fast a day in its place."
"7260","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A slave, then a camel, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of al-Zuhri."
